ForgeData.register("gcse-hist",{"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA":{"label":"Paper 1 — America, 1920–1973","color":"#9a3412","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-01","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Which development most directly enabled many Americans to buy expensive goods during the 1920s?","options":{"A":"Hire purchase","B":"Food rationing","C":"Nationalisation","D":"The New Deal"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-SOCIAL-CHANGE","scaffold":"Hire purchase allowed customers to pay in instalments, widening access to cars and household goods but also increasing debt.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did instalment buying make the 1920s boom vulnerable?","options":{"A":"It prevented firms from making profits","B":"It meant all goods were free","C":"Debt became hard to repay","D":"It banned consumer advertising"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-02","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What was a key feature of Ford's use of the assembly line?","options":{"A":"Each worker built a whole car","B":"It reduced production time and cost","C":"It ended the use of machinery","D":"It made cars available only to the rich"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-SOCIAL-CHANGE","scaffold":"Standardised tasks on a moving production line increased output and lowered the price of cars.","reforge":{"stem":"How did cheaper Ford cars contribute to wider economic growth?","options":{"A":"They reduced the need for roads","B":"They stopped people moving home","C":"They made all workers wealthy","D":"It boosted linked industries"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-03","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Why did Republican governments generally support business in the 1920s?","options":{"A":"They imposed high welfare spending","B":"They banned foreign investment","C":"Low taxes; limited regulation","D":"They controlled all factory output"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-SOCIAL-CHANGE","scaffold":"Republicans such as Harding, Coolidge and Hoover generally favoured laissez-faire policies, lower taxation and tariffs.","reforge":{"stem":"What was one likely effect of high US tariffs in the 1920s?","options":{"A":"Imports were made less competitive","B":"The USA joined the League of Nations","C":"American firms paid no wages","D":"Foreign goods became easier to sell in America"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-04","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Which group was least likely to share fully in the prosperity of the 1920s?","options":{"A":"Large car manufacturers","B":"Advertising agencies","C":"Urban cinema owners","D":"Many farmers"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-SOCIAL-CHANGE","scaffold":"Farmers faced overproduction, falling prices and debt even before the Wall Street Crash.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did overproduction damage many US farmers?","options":{"A":"It raised food prices sharply","B":"Surplus supply lowered prices","C":"It meant fewer crops were grown","D":"It prevented banks lending to industry"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-05","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What was the main purpose of prohibition from 1920?","options":{"A":"Alcohol ban","B":"To make alcohol cheaper","C":"To expand immigration","D":"To increase federal elections"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-SOCIAL-CHANGE","scaffold":"The Eighteenth Amendment prohibited alcohol, but enforcement was difficult and illegal supply grew.","reforge":{"stem":"Which consequence most weakened support for prohibition?","options":{"A":"The end of jazz music","B":"Lower city populations","C":"Organised crime","D":"A ban on newspapers"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-06","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Why did the Ku Klux Klan gain support during the 1920s?","options":{"A":"It promoted racial equality","B":"Prejudice and fear of change","C":"It defended immigrant rights","D":"It opposed Protestant values"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-SOCIAL-CHANGE","scaffold":"The Klan exploited racism, anti-Catholicism, anti-Semitism and anxiety about immigration and modern life.","reforge":{"stem":"Which development best shows anti-immigrant feeling in 1920s America?","options":{"A":"The New Deal","B":"The Marshall Plan","C":"The Civil Rights Act","D":"Immigration quotas"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-07","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Why was the Sacco and Vanzetti case significant?","options":{"A":"It created the Federal Reserve","B":"It ended prohibition","C":"Red Scare fears","D":"It gave women the vote"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-SOCIAL-CHANGE","scaffold":"Many people believed the two Italian anarchists were treated unfairly because of prejudice rather than evidence alone.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the Red Scare describe?","options":{"A":"Fear of communist influence","B":"A campaign for higher wages","C":"A farming protest","D":"A Hollywood film movement"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-08","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What was an important cause of the Wall Street Crash in 1929?","options":{"A":"A shortage of shares","B":"Too little factory production","C":"The end of radio broadcasts","D":"Borrowed share speculation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-DEPRESSION","scaffold":"Many investors bought shares on the margin, so a fall in prices left them unable to repay loans.","reforge":{"stem":"Why could a sharp fall in share prices affect ordinary businesses?","options":{"A":"It made every business state-owned","B":"Confidence and investment fell","C":"It guaranteed higher wages","D":"It ended all foreign trade"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-09","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Why did unemployment rise rapidly after the Wall Street Crash?","options":{"A":"Demand fell, so firms cut output","B":"The government banned hiring","C":"Farms stopped producing food","D":"Factories could not find workers"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-DEPRESSION","scaffold":"As consumers spent less, firms reduced production and dismissed workers; this deepened the Depression.","reforge":{"stem":"What was a 'Hooverville'?","options":{"A":"A military training camp","B":"A new government housing programme","C":"Settlement of homeless people","D":"A factory owned by Hoover"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-10","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Which criticism was most often made of Hoover's response to the Depression?","options":{"A":"He gave too much direct help too quickly","B":"Over-reliance on voluntary action","C":"He abolished all banks","D":"He introduced universal healthcare"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-DEPRESSION","scaffold":"Hoover encouraged cooperation and limited federal intervention, which appeared inadequate as hardship intensified.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did the Bonus Army protest damage Hoover's reputation?","options":{"A":"He accepted all its demands","B":"It led to a fall in tariffs","C":"It happened during the boom","D":"Removal of veterans"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-11","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What was Roosevelt's immediate aim in declaring a bank holiday in 1933?","options":{"A":"To raise alcohol taxes","B":"To close every business permanently","C":"To stop panic withdrawals","D":"To end presidential elections"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-DEPRESSION","scaffold":"The bank holiday temporarily closed banks while the government inspected them, helping restore confidence in the financial system.","reforge":{"stem":"Which phrase best summarises the New Deal's approach?","options":{"A":"Relief, recovery and reform","B":"Isolation, imperialism and intervention","C":"War, welfare and withdrawal","D":"Trade, tariffs and treaties"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-12","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Which New Deal measure was designed mainly to provide employment on public projects?","options":{"A":"The Eighteenth Amendment","B":"The Dawes Plan","C":"The Monroe Doctrine","D":"The CCC and WPA"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-DEPRESSION","scaffold":"Schemes such as the CCC and WPA gave jobs on environmental and public works projects, providing income and infrastructure.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did New Deal job schemes matter beyond wages?","options":{"A":"They abolished private business","B":"They improved public facilities","C":"They ended racial discrimination","D":"They guaranteed permanent jobs"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-13","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Why did some critics oppose the New Deal?","options":{"A":"Government intervention was too great","B":"They wanted fewer elections","C":"They opposed all road building","D":"They believed Roosevelt was too passive"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-DEPRESSION","scaffold":"Conservatives and Republicans criticised federal spending and regulation, while others argued the New Deal did not go far enough.","reforge":{"stem":"How did the Supreme Court limit parts of the New Deal?","options":{"A":"It required Roosevelt to resign","B":"It introduced new welfare laws","C":"Some measures were unconstitutional","D":"It ended the Depression immediately"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-14","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What was the most important reason the US economy recovered fully from the Depression?","options":{"A":"The League of Nations","B":"Wartime production","C":"The end of segregation","D":"The return of prohibition"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-DEPRESSION","scaffold":"The Second World War created enormous demand for US goods and labour, sharply reducing unemployment.","reforge":{"stem":"How did the Second World War change opportunities for many women?","options":{"A":"It removed women from paid work","B":"It banned factory employment","C":"It ended all discrimination","D":"It opened more industrial jobs"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-15","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did the term 'American Dream' suggest in post-war America?","options":{"A":"The government owned all homes","B":"All Americans had equal incomes","C":"Prosperity and social mobility","D":"Television was illegal"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-CIVIL-RIGHTS","scaffold":"Post-war consumer prosperity encouraged the idea that hard work could bring a home, car and higher living standards, though access was unequal.","reforge":{"stem":"Which product best symbolised 1950s US consumerism?","options":{"A":"A television set","B":"A ration book","C":"A gas mask","D":"A League covenant"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-16","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Why was McCarthyism controversial?","options":{"A":"It expanded civil rights","B":"It ended the Cold War","C":"It encouraged immigration","D":"Weak-evidence accusations"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-CIVIL-RIGHTS","scaffold":"Senator McCarthy exploited anti-communist fears, often making unsubstantiated accusations that damaged careers and civil liberties.","reforge":{"stem":"What was a likely effect of being blacklisted?","options":{"A":"Guaranteed election to office","B":"Lost employment opportunities","C":"Automatic imprisonment","D":"Free university education"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-17","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did Brown v. Board of Education challenge in 1954?","options":{"A":"School segregation","B":"The right to vote","C":"The New Deal","D":"Immigration quotas"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-CIVIL-RIGHTS","scaffold":"The Supreme Court ruled that segregated public schools were unconstitutional, challenging the legal basis of racial segregation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was the Little Rock crisis significant in 1957?","options":{"A":"It ended all segregation","B":"It began prohibition","C":"Federal enforcement","D":"It gave women equal pay"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-18","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Which method was most closely associated with Martin Luther King Jr?","options":{"A":"Armed revolution","B":"Non-violent direct action","C":"Support for segregation","D":"Opposition to voting rights"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-CIVIL-RIGHTS","scaffold":"King promoted non-violent protest, including boycotts, marches and sit-ins, to expose injustice and win support.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was television important to Civil Rights campaigns?","options":{"A":"It hid violence from the public","B":"It prevented marches occurring","C":"It replaced federal laws","D":"It broadcast racism widely"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-19","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did the Civil Rights Act of 1964 do?","options":{"A":"Abolished the presidency","B":"Ended the Vietnam War","C":"Public-place ban","D":"Created the KKK"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-CIVIL-RIGHTS","scaffold":"The Act prohibited discrimination in public places and employment, although enforcement and wider inequality remained issues.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the Voting Rights Act of 1965 target?","options":{"A":"Black voting access","B":"Consumer debt","C":"School attendance","D":"Immigration from Europe"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-20","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Why did some Black activists support Black Power in the late 1960s?","options":{"A":"They rejected all community action","B":"They believed segregation should return","C":"They opposed voting rights","D":"Self-determination and racial pride"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-CIVIL-RIGHTS","scaffold":"Black Power emphasised self-reliance, racial pride and control over Black communities, reflecting frustration with the pace of change.","reforge":{"stem":"Which issue showed limits to Civil Rights progress after new laws?","options":{"A":"A return to prohibition","B":"Inequality persisted","C":"The closure of Congress","D":"The end of music"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-21","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Why did radio and cinema matter to American society in the 1920s?","options":{"A":"They spread shared entertainment and consumer messages","B":"They ended advertising","C":"They were available only in rural areas, since electrification and radio-station coverage lagged behind cities.","D":"They replaced all newspapers"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-CULTURE","scaffold":"Mass media created national celebrities, spread popular culture and gave advertisers new ways to encourage consumption.","reforge":{"stem":"What did jazz symbolise to many young Americans?","options":{"A":"African-Americans received every benefit first","B":"The end of urban life","C":"Modern culture and changing social attitudes","D":"Support for prohibition"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-22","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What was one effect of the Nineteenth Amendment?","options":{"A":"Women were banned from factories","B":"Women gained the federal vote","C":"Prohibition ended","D":"The Supreme Court was abolished"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-CULTURE","scaffold":"The amendment gave women the constitutional right to vote, although discrimination and unequal access continued in practice.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did the flapper challenge some traditional attitudes?","options":{"A":"African-Americans received every benefit first","B":"She opposed all paid work","C":"She supported racial segregation","D":"She represented new fashions and independence"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-23","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Why did many African-Americans move north during the Great Migration?","options":{"A":"To avoid all factory employment","B":"To join the Confederate army","C":"To find industrial work and escape southern racism","D":"Britain and France planned to defend Abyssinia fully"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-CULTURE","scaffold":"Industrial jobs and the search for safety drew many African-Americans north, although discrimination and segregation remained there too.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the Great Migration help create in northern cities?","options":{"A":"Larger African-American communities","B":"They were available only in rural areas","C":"A rural population boom","D":"The closure of factories"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-24","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Why did the New Deal not end racial inequality?","options":{"A":"The Supreme Court ended segregation","B":"Roosevelt abolished civil rights laws","C":"African-Americans received every benefit first, since New Deal agencies prioritised Black communities over white ones.","D":"Many programmes were administered locally and discriminated"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-CULTURE","scaffold":"Some New Deal measures excluded agricultural and domestic workers or were administered in ways that disadvantaged African-Americans.","reforge":{"stem":"What was a limitation of the New Deal for women?","options":{"A":"It guaranteed equal pay","B":"Many programmes prioritised male breadwinners","C":"Foreign goods became easier to sell in America","D":"It ended women's employment"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-25","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did the Fair Deal attempt to do after 1945?","options":{"A":"Extend social and economic reforms","B":"Restore prohibition","C":"End federal responsibility","D":"Because Congress required relocation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-CULTURE","scaffold":"Truman's Fair Deal proposed expanding welfare and civil rights, although Congress blocked or limited many measures.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was the GI Bill important after the Second World War?","options":{"A":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","B":"It ended television","C":"It helped veterans access education and housing","D":"It created the KKK"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-26","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Why did suburbanisation grow in post-war America?","options":{"A":"Cities banned all families","B":"Cars, mortgages and new housing encouraged movement","C":"The government ended road building","D":"Britain and France planned to defend Abyssinia fully"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-CULTURE","scaffold":"Mass car ownership, highway construction and affordable mortgages supported movement to suburbs, though racial exclusion was common.","reforge":{"stem":"How could suburban growth reinforce inequality?","options":{"A":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","B":"It ended segregation in cities","C":"It stopped consumerism","D":"Housing discrimination excluded many Black families"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-27","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Why did the Montgomery Bus Boycott succeed?","options":{"A":"Bus companies supported segregation","B":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","C":"It used sustained organisation and economic pressure","D":"It lasted only one afternoon"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-RIGHTS-CASES","scaffold":"The boycott lasted over a year and combined local organisation, car pools and financial pressure with legal action.","reforge":{"stem":"What immediate issue triggered the Montgomery boycott?","options":{"A":"Rosa Parks's arrest","B":"The Wall Street Crash","C":"The Cuban Missile Crisis","D":"The New Deal"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-28","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did the Freedom Rides aim to test?","options":{"A":"Whether federal New Deal relief programmes had funded new schools equally across the segregated South.","B":"Whether women could vote","C":"Whether prohibition had ended","D":"Whether interstate bus segregation rules were enforced"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-RIGHTS-CASES","scaffold":"Black and white activists travelled together to challenge segregation in interstate bus facilities.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Freedom Riders attract national attention?","options":{"A":"They supported segregation","B":"Violence was shown in the media","C":"They travelled only abroad","D":"Bus companies supported segregation"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-29","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Why did the Great Society have mixed results?","options":{"A":"It reduced some poverty but could not remove structural inequality","B":"It provided no social programmes","C":"It ended after one month","D":"It focused only on foreign policy and international trade agreements, ignoring domestic reform entirely."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-RIGHTS-CASES","scaffold":"Medicare, Medicaid and anti-poverty programmes improved access for many, but poverty, racial inequality and opposition limited their impact.","reforge":{"stem":"What was one Great Society achievement?","options":{"A":"The end of all poverty","B":"A return to segregation","C":"Medicare for older Americans","D":"The Supreme Court ordered it on day one"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-30","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did Roe v Wade decide in 1973?","options":{"A":"Women could not work","B":"The Constitution protected a right to abortion in certain circumstances","C":"Segregation was constitutional under the doctrine established decades earlier in Plessy v Ferguson.","D":"The New Deal had ended"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-RIGHTS-CASES","scaffold":"The Supreme Court ruling recognised a constitutional privacy right that limited state abortion bans, though it remained controversial.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Roe v Wade become a major political controversy?","options":{"A":"It ended the Civil Rights Movement by resolving every remaining dispute over racial equality.","B":"It decided a presidential election","C":"It created the Federal Reserve","D":"It affected competing views about rights and state power"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-31","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What helped create a national consumer culture in 1920s America?","options":{"A":"Demand for housing and schools","B":"Pensions and welfare support","C":"Chain stores","D":"Segregated lunch counters"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-ECONOMIC-STATE","scaffold":"Chain stores such as Woolworths and A&P used mass advertising and standardised pricing to sell the same goods nationwide, helping create a shared consumer culture across the USA.","reforge":{"stem":"What kind of business expanded consumer choice across the country?","options":{"A":"Chain stores","B":"Farm prices","C":"Education and mortgages","D":"Execution for espionage"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-32","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Which group was hit by falling cotton prices?","options":{"A":"The New Deal did not go far enough","B":"Civil rights legislation and jobs","C":"Mass-produced suburban homes","D":"Southern farmers"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-ECONOMIC-STATE","scaffold":"Cotton prices collapsed through the 1920s as overproduction and competition from synthetic fibres drove prices down, leaving Southern farmers, especially sharecroppers, in deep poverty even before the Wall Street Crash.","reforge":{"stem":"Who suffered when agricultural prices fell?","options":{"A":"Tennessee Valley Authority","B":"Southern farmers","C":"Trade-union power","D":"Separate but equal segregation"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-33","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did the Emergency Banking Act help restore?","options":{"A":"Confidence in banks","B":"Reduced it sharply","C":"Television","D":"Housing discrimination"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-ECONOMIC-STATE","scaffold":"Roosevelt's 1933 Emergency Banking Act closed weak banks and reopened only the sound ones under federal inspection, restoring public confidence and ending the bank runs of early 1933.","reforge":{"stem":"What was the immediate aim of Roosevelt's banking legislation?","options":{"A":"Segregated lunch counters","B":"Pensions and welfare support","C":"Confidence in banks","D":"Demand for housing and schools"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-34","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did the Agricultural Adjustment Act attempt to raise?","options":{"A":"Black separatism and self-defence","B":"Farm prices","C":"Alleged communist influence","D":"African-American workers"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-ECONOMIC-STATE","scaffold":"The Agricultural Adjustment Act paid farmers to reduce production, aiming to cut the surplus that had driven prices down and raise farmers' incomes.","reforge":{"stem":"What was the AAA designed to improve?","options":{"A":"The New Deal did not go far enough","B":"Civil rights legislation and jobs","C":"Mass-produced suburban homes","D":"Farm prices"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-35","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Which New Deal agency provided electricity in rural areas?","options":{"A":"Demand for housing and schools","B":"Education and mortgages","C":"Tennessee Valley Authority","D":"Equal rights for women"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-ECONOMIC-STATE","scaffold":"The Tennessee Valley Authority built dams to control flooding and generate hydroelectric power, bringing electricity to rural areas of the South that private companies had found unprofitable to serve.","reforge":{"stem":"Which agency built dams and supplied regional power?","options":{"A":"Tennessee Valley Authority","B":"Reduced it sharply","C":"Television","D":"Pensions and welfare support"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-36","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did Social Security provide from 1935?","options":{"A":"Trade-union power","B":"Unequal pay by sex","C":"Separate but equal segregation","D":"Pensions and welfare support"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-ECONOMIC-STATE","scaffold":"The Social Security Act of 1935 introduced old-age pensions and unemployment insurance, creating the first federal welfare safety net in the USA.","reforge":{"stem":"Which reform offered support in old age and unemployment?","options":{"A":"Alleged communist influence","B":"Pensions and welfare support","C":"African-American workers","D":"Black separatism and self-defence"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-37","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Why did Roosevelt face criticism from the left?","options":{"A":"The New Deal did not go far enough","B":"Demand for housing and schools","C":"Segregated lunch counters","D":"It prevented firms from making profits"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-ECONOMIC-STATE","scaffold":"Critics like Huey Long argued the New Deal left too much wealth in the hands of the rich and did not redistribute income or property radically enough to end poverty.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did radical critics attack Roosevelt's reforms?","options":{"A":"It made cars available only to the rich","B":"Education and mortgages","C":"The New Deal did not go far enough","D":"Equal rights for women"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-38","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did wartime production do to US unemployment?","options":{"A":"Chain stores","B":"Reduced it sharply","C":"Civil rights legislation and jobs","D":"Mass-produced suburban homes"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-ECONOMIC-STATE","scaffold":"Wartime factories needed millions of workers to produce weapons, vehicles and supplies, cutting unemployment from around 15% in 1940 to under 2% by 1944.","reforge":{"stem":"How did mobilisation affect jobs?","options":{"A":"Unequal pay by sex","B":"Trade-union power","C":"Separate but equal segregation","D":"Reduced it sharply"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-39","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Which group gained industrial opportunities during the war?","options":{"A":"Alleged communist influence","B":"Television","C":"African-American workers","D":"Southern farmers"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-WAR-COLD-WAR","scaffold":"Labour shortages during the war opened up better-paid factory jobs to African-Americans, especially in Northern cities, though discrimination in pay and promotion continued.","reforge":{"stem":"Who entered more wartime industrial jobs?","options":{"A":"African-American workers","B":"Demand for housing and schools","C":"Segregated lunch counters","D":"Opposition and continuing poverty"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-40","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did the GI Bill help many veterans obtain?","options":{"A":"Alleged communist influence","B":"Confidence in banks","C":"Black separatism and self-defence","D":"Education and mortgages"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-WAR-COLD-WAR","scaffold":"The 1944 GI Bill gave veterans funded access to college education and low-interest mortgages, helping fuel the postwar growth of the middle class and suburbs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which post-war benefits supported veterans?","options":{"A":"Mass-produced suburban homes","B":"Education and mortgages","C":"Civil rights legislation and jobs","D":"Chain stores"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-41","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did the 1947 Taft-Hartley Act restrict?","options":{"A":"Trade-union power","B":"Execution for espionage","C":"Equal rights for women","D":"Farm prices"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-WAR-COLD-WAR","scaffold":"The Taft-Hartley Act banned closed shops and allowed states to pass 'right-to-work' laws, weakening the bargaining power unions had gained during the New Deal.","reforge":{"stem":"Which law limited organised labour?","options":{"A":"Housing discrimination","B":"Television","C":"Trade-union power","D":"Southern farmers"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-42","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did the baby boom increase after 1945?","options":{"A":"Tennessee Valley Authority","B":"Demand for housing and schools","C":"Unequal pay by sex","D":"Separate but equal segregation"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-WAR-COLD-WAR","scaffold":"Rising birth rates after 1945 created a huge demand for family housing, schools and consumer goods, driving the rapid growth of suburbs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which demographic change increased pressure on services?","options":{"A":"Confidence in banks","B":"Alleged communist influence","C":"Black separatism and self-defence","D":"Demand for housing and schools"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-43","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What was the key feature of Levittown?","options":{"A":"Opposition and continuing poverty","B":"Segregated lunch counters","C":"Mass-produced suburban homes","D":"Pensions and welfare support"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-WAR-COLD-WAR","scaffold":"Levittown used assembly-line construction methods to build thousands of near-identical, affordable homes quickly, becoming the model for postwar suburban development.","reforge":{"stem":"What represented post-war suburban expansion?","options":{"A":"Mass-produced suburban homes","B":"Black separatism and self-defence","C":"Equal rights for women","D":"Farm prices"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-44","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"Which medium helped spread rock and roll?","options":{"A":"Civil rights legislation and jobs","B":"The New Deal did not go far enough","C":"Chain stores","D":"Television"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-WAR-COLD-WAR","scaffold":"Television brought performers like Elvis Presley into millions of homes, spreading rock and roll to a national teenage audience far faster than radio or records alone.","reforge":{"stem":"What helped popular culture reach national audiences?","options":{"A":"Separate but equal segregation","B":"Television","C":"Unequal pay by sex","D":"Tennessee Valley Authority"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-45","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did the House Un-American Activities Committee investigate?","options":{"A":"Alleged communist influence","B":"Mass-produced suburban homes","C":"Southern farmers","D":"Reduced it sharply"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-WAR-COLD-WAR","scaffold":"HUAC investigated individuals and organisations, especially in Hollywood, suspected of communist sympathies, part of the wider Cold War anti-communist panic.","reforge":{"stem":"Which committee investigated suspected subversion?","options":{"A":"Segregated lunch counters","B":"Opposition and continuing poverty","C":"Alleged communist influence","D":"Pensions and welfare support"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-46","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What was the outcome of the Rosenberg case?","options":{"A":"African-American workers","B":"Execution for espionage","C":"Confidence in banks","D":"Black separatism and self-defence"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-WAR-COLD-WAR","scaffold":"Julius and Ethel Rosenberg were convicted of passing atomic secrets to the Soviet Union and executed in 1953, reflecting Cold War fears about communist infiltration.","reforge":{"stem":"What happened to Julius and Ethel Rosenberg?","options":{"A":"Chain stores","B":"Civil rights legislation and jobs","C":"The New Deal did not go far enough","D":"Execution for espionage"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-47","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did Plessy v Ferguson support?","options":{"A":"Farm prices","B":"Equal rights for women","C":"Separate but equal segregation","D":"Opposition and continuing poverty"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-EQUALITY","scaffold":"The 1896 Supreme Court ruling in Plessy v Ferguson upheld racial segregation as constitutional provided facilities were officially 'equal', legalising Jim Crow laws across the South.","reforge":{"stem":"Which ruling provided a legal basis for segregation?","options":{"A":"Separate but equal segregation","B":"The New Deal did not go far enough","C":"Southern farmers","D":"Reduced it sharply"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-48","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did the Greensboro sit-ins challenge?","options":{"A":"Unequal pay by sex","B":"Trade-union power","C":"Tennessee Valley Authority","D":"Segregated lunch counters"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-EQUALITY","scaffold":"Four Black students sat at a whites-only Woolworths lunch counter in Greensboro in 1960, directly challenging segregation in public spaces through peaceful protest.","reforge":{"stem":"Which facility did the 1960 sit-ins target?","options":{"A":"African-American workers","B":"Segregated lunch counters","C":"Confidence in banks","D":"Black separatism and self-defence"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-49","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What was the aim of the March on Washington?","options":{"A":"Civil rights legislation and jobs","B":"Opposition and continuing poverty","C":"Pensions and welfare support","D":"Demand for housing and schools"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-EQUALITY","scaffold":"The 1963 March on Washington called for the passage of civil rights legislation and greater employment opportunities for African-Americans, and included Martin Luther King's 'I Have a Dream' speech.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the 1963 march demand?","options":{"A":"The New Deal did not go far enough","B":"Equal rights for women","C":"Civil rights legislation and jobs","D":"Farm prices"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-50","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did the Civil Rights Act of 1968 address?","options":{"A":"Mass-produced suburban homes","B":"Housing discrimination","C":"The New Deal did not go far enough","D":"Chain stores"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-EQUALITY","scaffold":"The Civil Rights Act of 1968 (Fair Housing Act) banned discrimination in the sale or rental of housing based on race, following King's assassination.","reforge":{"stem":"Which discrimination did the 1968 Act target?","options":{"A":"Unequal pay by sex","B":"Trade-union power","C":"Tennessee Valley Authority","D":"Housing discrimination"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-51","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did Malcolm X initially advocate?","options":{"A":"The New Deal did not go far enough","B":"Southern farmers","C":"Black separatism and self-defence","D":"Television"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-EQUALITY","scaffold":"Malcolm X, influenced by the Nation of Islam, argued Black Americans should build separate institutions and defend themselves rather than seek integration, before later moderating his views.","reforge":{"stem":"Which ideas were associated with Malcolm X's early politics?","options":{"A":"Black separatism and self-defence","B":"Opposition and continuing poverty","C":"Pensions and welfare support","D":"Demand for housing and schools"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-52","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did the National Organisation for Women campaign for?","options":{"A":"Confidence in banks","B":"Alleged communist influence","C":"African-American workers","D":"Equal rights for women"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-EQUALITY","scaffold":"Founded in 1966, the National Organisation for Women campaigned for equal pay, reproductive rights and an end to sex discrimination in employment and education.","reforge":{"stem":"Which organisation promoted women's equality?","options":{"A":"Chain stores","B":"Equal rights for women","C":"The New Deal did not go far enough","D":"Mass-produced suburban homes"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-53","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What did the Equal Pay Act of 1963 target?","options":{"A":"Unequal pay by sex","B":"Farm prices","C":"Education and mortgages","D":"Execution for espionage"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-EQUALITY","scaffold":"The Equal Pay Act made it illegal to pay women less than men for the same job, though a real pay gap persisted for decades afterwards.","reforge":{"stem":"Which workplace inequality did the Act address?","options":{"A":"Television","B":"Southern farmers","C":"Unequal pay by sex","D":"Reduced it sharply"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-AM-54","spec":"GCSE-HIST-AMERICA","stem":"What was one limitation of the Great Society?","options":{"A":"The New Deal did not go far enough","B":"Opposition and continuing poverty","C":"Trade-union power","D":"Tennessee Valley Authority"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-AM-EQUALITY","scaffold":"Johnson's Great Society programmes reduced poverty rates and expanded healthcare through Medicare and Medicaid, but faced funding cuts as the Vietnam War escalated and did not eliminate poverty.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Great Society reforms not solve every problem?","options":{"A":"Confidence in banks","B":"They imposed high welfare spending","C":"African-American workers","D":"Opposition and continuing poverty"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-america"}]},"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR":{"label":"Paper 1 — Inter-war Years, 1918–1939","color":"#7c2d12","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-01","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What was Wilson's main aim in proposing the Fourteen Points?","options":{"A":"Fair peace and self-determination","B":"Permanent punishment of Germany","C":"French control of the Rhineland","D":"British ownership of all colonies"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-SETTLEMENT","scaffold":"Wilson hoped for a lasting peace based on principles such as self-determination, open diplomacy and a League of Nations.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Clemenceau disagree with Wilson's approach?","options":{"A":"France had not fought in the war","B":"He wanted Germany strengthened","C":"Security against Germany","D":"He opposed all reparations"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-02","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did many Germans call the Treaty of Versailles a 'Diktat'?","options":{"A":"Germany chose its own terms","B":"Imposed terms","C":"It gave Germany new colonies","D":"It ended reparations"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-SETTLEMENT","scaffold":"Germany was not allowed to negotiate the final terms and many Germans regarded the treaty as an imposed settlement.","reforge":{"stem":"Which Treaty term was especially resented because it blamed Germany for the war?","options":{"A":"The Locarno Pact","B":"The Stresa Front","C":"The Kellogg-Briand Pact","D":"Article 231"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-03","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What was the immediate purpose of reparations?","options":{"A":"To create the League of Nations","B":"To divide Germany into zones","C":"Allied compensation","D":"To end conscription in Britain"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-SETTLEMENT","scaffold":"Reparations were payments intended to compensate the Allies for wartime damage, but they became a major source of German resentment.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did reparations create political problems in Germany?","options":{"A":"They were seen as humiliating and costly","B":"They stopped inflation immediately","C":"They gave Germany more territory","D":"They strengthened support for the treaty"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-04","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Which was a weakness of the League of Nations?","options":{"A":"It had no member states","B":"It had no written covenant","C":"It had no headquarters","D":"No armed force of its own"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-SETTLEMENT","scaffold":"The League depended on member states to apply sanctions or use force, making action difficult when major powers disagreed.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did US absence weaken the League?","options":{"A":"The USA was not economically important","B":"It removed a major potential power","C":"It stopped Britain joining","D":"It ended the Covenant"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-05","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why was the League's decision over the Aaland Islands seen as a success?","options":{"A":"Accepted settlement","B":"It used military conquest","C":"It gave islands to Germany","D":"It ended the Depression"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-SETTLEMENT","scaffold":"The League investigated the dispute and its decision was accepted by Sweden and Finland, demonstrating cooperation in the 1920s.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the Aaland Islands case suggest about League success?","options":{"A":"It succeeded only in global wars","B":"It always acted without delay","C":"Small-dispute success","D":"It had a powerful army"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-06","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did the League fail to stop Japan in Manchuria in 1931?","options":{"A":"Japan was not involved","B":"Its response was slow and weak","C":"The crisis took place in Europe","D":"The USA ordered an invasion"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-SETTLEMENT","scaffold":"The Lytton Report took about a year and the League imposed no effective sanctions; Japan simply left the League.","reforge":{"stem":"What did Japan's withdrawal from the League show?","options":{"A":"The League had gained power","B":"The crisis was fully resolved","C":"Sanctions had worked quickly","D":"League could be defied"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-07","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did economic sanctions against Italy over Abyssinia have limited effect?","options":{"A":"The League had no members","B":"Italy had no trade links","C":"Oil not sanctioned","D":"Abyssinia supported Italy"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-SETTLEMENT","scaffold":"Britain and France avoided measures likely to provoke Italy, notably an oil embargo and closure of the Suez Canal.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the Hoare-Laval Pact reveal?","options":{"A":"Britain and France considered sacrificing Abyssinia","B":"The League intended to remove Mussolini","C":"Italy had agreed to withdraw","D":"Britain and France planned to defend Abyssinia fully"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-08","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did the Locarno Treaties of 1925 appear to achieve?","options":{"A":"German rearmament","B":"A Soviet invasion of Poland","C":"The end of the League","D":"Western cooperation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ROAD-TO-WAR","scaffold":"Locarno helped stabilise western borders and encouraged a more cooperative 'spirit of Locarno' during the 1920s.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was Germany joining the League in 1926 significant?","options":{"A":"It ended all territorial disputes","B":"Greater cooperation","C":"It made Hitler chancellor","D":"It cancelled the Treaty of Versailles"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-09","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did Hitler remilitarise the Rhineland in 1936?","options":{"A":"Revise Versailles","B":"It gave France control of Germany","C":"It was required by the League","D":"It ended the Nazi-Soviet Pact"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ROAD-TO-WAR","scaffold":"Remilitarisation broke Versailles and Locarno terms; weak Allied reaction encouraged Hitler to take further risks.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did lack of military response to Rhineland remilitarisation matter?","options":{"A":"It strengthened the League army","B":"It ended German rearmament","C":"Further aggression","D":"It removed Hitler from power"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-10","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What was Anschluss in 1938?","options":{"A":"Occupation of Poland","B":"German-Austrian union","C":"A British-French alliance","D":"A League sanctions policy"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ROAD-TO-WAR","scaffold":"Germany's annexation of Austria violated the Versailles settlement and showed the growing failure of collective security.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Anschluss alarm opponents of Nazi Germany?","options":{"A":"It weakened Germany","B":"It ended appeasement","C":"It restored Austrian independence","D":"It expanded German power"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-11","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did Hitler demand in the Sudeten crisis?","options":{"A":"Control of the Suez Canal","B":"A new League covenant","C":"Sudetenland transfer","D":"The removal of Mussolini"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ROAD-TO-WAR","scaffold":"Hitler claimed to protect ethnic Germans in Czechoslovakia, using the Sudetenland crisis to press territorial demands.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the Munich Agreement allow Germany to take?","options":{"A":"Sudetenland","B":"Poland","C":"The Rhineland","D":"Abyssinia"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-12","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did Chamberlain initially support appeasement?","options":{"A":"He supported fascism in all countries","B":"He wanted war with Germany","C":"Britain had no armed forces","D":"Some German grievances seemed valid"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ROAD-TO-WAR","scaffold":"Appeasement was influenced by memories of the First World War, military weakness, economic pressures and a belief that some treaty terms were harsh.","reforge":{"stem":"Which event showed that Hitler's aims went beyond uniting German-speaking people?","options":{"A":"The Saar plebiscite","B":"Czechoslovakia occupied","C":"The Kellogg-Briand Pact","D":"The Aaland Islands decision"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-13","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why was the Nazi-Soviet Pact significant in August 1939?","options":{"A":"Avoided a two-front war","B":"It joined the USSR to the League","C":"It made Poland independent","D":"It forced Britain to surrender"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ROAD-TO-WAR","scaffold":"The pact included a secret agreement to divide eastern Europe and allowed Hitler to invade Poland without immediate Soviet opposition.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did the Nazi-Soviet Pact shock many observers?","options":{"A":"Both states had been close allies","B":"It gave Britain Poland","C":"It joined ideological enemies","D":"It ended all European alliances"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-14","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What directly triggered the outbreak of war in September 1939?","options":{"A":"Italy invaded Abyssinia","B":"Germany invaded Poland","C":"Japan invaded Manchuria","D":"Germany entered the Rhineland"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ROAD-TO-WAR","scaffold":"Britain and France had guaranteed Poland's security; after Germany invaded, they declared war on 3 September 1939.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Britain guarantee Poland in 1939?","options":{"A":"It wanted to restore the League","B":"It supported the Nazi-Soviet Pact","C":"It planned to invade the USSR","D":"Deter German expansion"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-15","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Which judgement best explains the outbreak of the Second World War?","options":{"A":"Only Chamberlain was responsible","B":"Only Stalin was responsible","C":"Aggression plus wider failures","D":"Only the League caused the war"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ROAD-TO-WAR","scaffold":"Hitler's expansionism was central, but League weakness, appeasement, economic problems and diplomacy also shaped the path to war.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a multi-causal explanation stronger than blaming one event alone?","options":{"A":"Interacting causes","B":"It avoids using evidence","C":"It ignores individual decisions","D":"It proves all causes were equal"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-16","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did the Saar plebiscite of 1935 demonstrate?","options":{"A":"British withdrawal from Europe","B":"A League invasion of Germany","C":"Italian control of Austria","D":"Support for German rule"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-DICTATORSHIP","scaffold":"Over 90% voted for the Saar to return to Germany, giving Hitler a popular and legal propaganda success.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Hitler use the Saar result in propaganda?","options":{"A":"It proved France had invaded Germany","B":"German national unity","C":"It ended rearmament","D":"It guaranteed peace with Britain"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-17","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why was the Anglo-German Naval Agreement controversial in 1935?","options":{"A":"It allowed German naval expansion","B":"It strengthened the League's sanctions","C":"It limited Britain to no navy","D":"It ended German conscription"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-DICTATORSHIP","scaffold":"Britain allowed Germany a navy up to 35% of British tonnage, undermining the united Stresa Front response to German rearmament.","reforge":{"stem":"How did the agreement affect relations with France and Italy?","options":{"A":"It strengthened the Stresa Front","B":"It increased League membership","C":"Weakened anti-German cooperation","D":"It ended fascism in Italy"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-18","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did the Stresa Front oppose?","options":{"A":"The League of Nations","B":"German rearmament","C":"French security","D":"Polish independence"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-DICTATORSHIP","scaffold":"Britain, France and Italy agreed at Stresa in 1935 to oppose German treaty breaches, but the front soon collapsed.","reforge":{"stem":"Which event most directly weakened the Stresa Front?","options":{"A":"The Aaland Islands dispute","B":"The Dawes Plan","C":"The Locarno Treaties","D":"Naval Agreement"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-19","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did the Depression make international peace harder to maintain?","options":{"A":"It increased trust between states","B":"It removed extremist parties","C":"Nationalism and aggression","D":"It made the League's army stronger"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-DICTATORSHIP","scaffold":"Economic hardship made cooperation harder and helped extremist leaders gain support by promising expansion or national revival.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did countries become less willing to use sanctions during the Depression?","options":{"A":"Economic self-protection","B":"The League banned sanctions","C":"Trade restrictions had no effect","D":"Japan controlled all trade"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-20","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What was a major limitation of the Kellogg-Briand Pact?","options":{"A":"It made war legal","B":"It had no promise to avoid war","C":"It applied only to Germany","D":"No way to enforce its promise"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-DICTATORSHIP","scaffold":"States promised to renounce war, but the pact had no enforcement mechanism and did not prevent later aggression.","reforge":{"stem":"Which criticism applies to both Kellogg-Briand and the League?","options":{"A":"Neither made any peace agreement","B":"Weak enforcement","C":"Both excluded Britain and France","D":"Both were created after 1939"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-21","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did the Treaty of Versailles create problems for new states?","options":{"A":"Borders left minorities and territorial disputes","B":"Hitler was central but other failures contributed","C":"It abolished nationalism","D":"It made all borders temporary"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-DICTATORSHIP","scaffold":"Self-determination could not be applied consistently, leaving minorities outside their preferred state and creating grievances.","reforge":{"stem":"What was one result of redrawing eastern European borders?","options":{"A":"The League gained an army","B":"All disputes disappeared","C":"New minorities lived inside several states","D":"They created financial and political resentment"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-22","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did the League's agencies count as a strength?","options":{"A":"Britain and France planned to defend Abyssinia fully","B":"They achieved practical work in health and refugees","C":"They ended all poverty","D":"They commanded large armies"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-DICTATORSHIP","scaffold":"Agencies such as the Health Organisation and Refugee Organisation achieved useful cooperation even when political action failed.","reforge":{"stem":"What limited the League's agencies?","options":{"A":"Hitler was central but other failures contributed","B":"They were created in 1939","C":"They had no practical purpose","D":"They depended on member cooperation and funding"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-23","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did Britain and France hesitate to confront Italy over Abyssinia?","options":{"A":"They had no interest in Europe","B":"They wanted Italy to conquer Britain","C":"They feared pushing Mussolini towards Hitler","D":"They created financial and political resentment"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-APPEASEMENT","scaffold":"British and French leaders wanted Italian support against Germany, so they prioritised diplomacy over decisive sanctions.","reforge":{"stem":"What was the consequence of the Abyssinian crisis for collective security?","options":{"A":"Aggressors saw that League opposition could be resisted","B":"The League became militarily stronger after member states pooled their armed forces against Italy.","C":"Italy left fascism","D":"Germany withdrew from Europe"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-24","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did Hitler withdraw Germany from the League in 1933?","options":{"A":"He wanted to join the League later, once German rearmament had already been completed.","B":"France demanded German membership","C":"The League had invaded Germany","D":"He rejected international restrictions and cooperation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-APPEASEMENT","scaffold":"Withdrawal allowed Hitler to reject collective security while pursuing rearmament and revision of Versailles.","reforge":{"stem":"How did German rearmament challenge Versailles?","options":{"A":"It strengthened the League","B":"It broke military restrictions","C":"It reduced Germany's army","D":"It returned colonies to Britain"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-25","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did Hitler believe Britain and France might not resist expansion?","options":{"A":"Their weak responses to earlier treaty breaches encouraged him","B":"They had already declared their full support for German invasion of neighbouring states.","C":"They had no armed forces at all","D":"The League had already surrendered"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-APPEASEMENT","scaffold":"The lack of decisive action over the Rhineland, Anschluss and Munich encouraged Hitler to believe opponents lacked the will to fight.","reforge":{"stem":"Which earlier crisis most clearly tested Allied resolve before Munich?","options":{"A":"The Dawes Plan","B":"The Aaland Islands","C":"The remilitarisation of the Rhineland","D":"They supported the League's military force"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-26","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did the Munich Agreement fail to secure lasting peace?","options":{"A":"It made Poland a German ally","B":"It rewarded pressure and left Czechoslovakia vulnerable","C":"It gave the League an army","D":"It removed all German ambitions to expand beyond territories where German speakers lived."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-APPEASEMENT","scaffold":"Munich transferred the Sudetenland but did not satisfy Hitler's wider ambitions; Germany occupied the rest of Czechoslovakia in 1939.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the occupation of Prague in March 1939 change?","options":{"A":"It made appeasement more popular with the British public, who welcomed further concessions.","B":"It ended German expansion","C":"It restored the League","D":"It undermined the claim that Hitler only wanted German-speaking lands"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-27","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did Poland reject Hitler's demand for Danzig?","options":{"A":"It had no access to the Baltic Sea and depended entirely on the corridor for trade.","B":"It wanted to join the USSR","C":"It feared loss of sovereignty and further German demands","D":"Britain had invaded Poland"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-APPEASEMENT","scaffold":"Danzig and the Polish Corridor had strategic and symbolic importance, while Poland feared surrender would invite further demands.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the British guarantee to Poland aim to do?","options":{"A":"Deter German attack","B":"Support the Nazi-Soviet Pact","C":"End Polish independence","D":"Give Danzig to Germany"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-28","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did the Soviet Union sign the Nazi-Soviet Pact?","options":{"A":"It accepted Nazi ideology","B":"It ended Soviet communism","C":"They created financial and political resentment","D":"It bought time and offered territorial gains"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-APPEASEMENT","scaffold":"Stalin gained time to prepare and expected territorial concessions in eastern Europe, including a division of Poland.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the pact's secret protocol do?","options":{"A":"Created NATO","B":"Divided spheres of influence in eastern Europe","C":"Hitler was central but other failures contributed","D":"Ended German rearmament"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-29","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did the failure of collective security contribute to war?","options":{"A":"Aggressors were not consistently deterred","B":"They supported the League's military force","C":"It ended nationalism","D":"It prevented rearmament"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-APPEASEMENT","scaffold":"Repeated failures in Manchuria, Abyssinia and Europe undermined confidence that international cooperation could restrain aggression.","reforge":{"stem":"What is collective security intended to mean?","options":{"A":"They strengthened support for the treaty","B":"Each state fights alone","C":"States act together against aggression","D":"Trade replaces diplomacy"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-30","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Which factor best explains why war broke out in 1939 rather than earlier?","options":{"A":"Poland invited invasion","B":"Hitler's risks increased as appeasement delayed confrontation","C":"The League had defeated Japan","D":"Germany had abandoned expansion after the failure of the Munich Agreement negotiations."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ALLIANCES","scaffold":"Appeasement postponed direct conflict while allowing German strength and ambitions to grow; the invasion of Poland finally triggered declarations of war.","reforge":{"stem":"What immediate promise did Britain make to Poland in 1939?","options":{"A":"A report condemning Japan's action","B":"To cede Danzig","C":"To leave Europe permanently","D":"To support Poland if attacked"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-31","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What principle did Wilson's Fourteen Points promote?","options":{"A":"A report condemning Japan's action","B":"The United States","C":"National self-determination","D":"The annexation of Austria"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ALLIANCES","scaffold":"Wilson's Fourteen Points argued that ethnic and national groups should have the right to govern themselves, a principle used to redraw the map of Central and Eastern Europe after 1918.","reforge":{"stem":"Which principle shaped Wilson's peace aims?","options":{"A":"National self-determination","B":"They created financial and political resentment","C":"States prioritised national interests","D":"The Treaty of Versailles"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-32","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did France gain from the Treaty settlement?","options":{"A":"Peace through concessions","B":"Sanctions","C":"Modern weapons and air power","D":"Alsace-Lorraine"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ALLIANCES","scaffold":"France regained Alsace-Lorraine, territory lost to Germany in 1871, as part of the Treaty of Versailles settlement.","reforge":{"stem":"Which territory returned to France?","options":{"A":"Its founding agreement","B":"Alsace-Lorraine","C":"Manchuria's resources and territory","D":"A previously demilitarised zone"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-33","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What happened to Germany's armed forces?","options":{"A":"They were limited","B":"A plebiscite and partition","C":"Germany and Italy","D":"Czechoslovakia from negotiations"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ALLIANCES","scaffold":"The Treaty of Versailles capped the German army at 100,000 men, banned tanks and military aircraft, and reduced the navy, intended to prevent Germany rearming for another war.","reforge":{"stem":"How did Versailles restrict German military power?","options":{"A":"A report condemning Japan's action","B":"The United States","C":"They were limited","D":"The annexation of Austria"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-34","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did Germany oppose reparations?","options":{"A":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","B":"They created financial and political resentment","C":"Communism","D":"The rest of Czechoslovakia"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ALLIANCES","scaffold":"Germany was forced to accept blame for the war (the 'War Guilt' clause) and pay reparations, which many Germans saw as unfairly harsh and used to fuel resentment against the Treaty.","reforge":{"stem":"Why were reparations unpopular in Germany?","options":{"A":"Britain and France planned to defend Abyssinia fully","B":"Sanctions","C":"Peace through concessions","D":"They created financial and political resentment"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-35","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What was the League's Covenant?","options":{"A":"The Treaty of Versailles","B":"States prioritised national interests","C":"Its founding agreement","D":"Invade Poland without Soviet opposition"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ALLIANCES","scaffold":"The Covenant set out the League of Nations' aims and rules, including collective security and the use of sanctions against aggressor states.","reforge":{"stem":"What document established the League's aims and rules?","options":{"A":"Its founding agreement","B":"A plebiscite and partition","C":"Germany and Italy","D":"Czechoslovakia from negotiations"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-36","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Which power was absent from the League?","options":{"A":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","B":"Manchuria's resources and territory","C":"A previously demilitarised zone","D":"The United States"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-ALLIANCES","scaffold":"The US Senate refused to ratify the Treaty of Versailles, so the USA never joined the League, weakening its authority since one of the world's most powerful nations was absent.","reforge":{"stem":"Which major country did not join?","options":{"A":"War as an instrument of policy","B":"The United States","C":"Communism","D":"The rest of Czechoslovakia"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-37","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What was the League's main method of pressure?","options":{"A":"Sanctions","B":"A report condemning Japan's action","C":"The annexation of Austria","D":"Hitler was central but other failures contributed"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-WAR-CAUSES","scaffold":"The League's main tool against aggression was economic sanctions, restricting trade with an aggressor state, since it had no army of its own to enforce decisions militarily.","reforge":{"stem":"How could the League punish an aggressor economically?","options":{"A":"Invade Poland without Soviet opposition","B":"States prioritised national interests","C":"Sanctions","D":"The Treaty of Versailles"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-38","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did the League decide in the Upper Silesia dispute?","options":{"A":"Modern weapons and air power","B":"A plebiscite and partition","C":"Peace through concessions","D":"National self-determination"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-WAR-CAUSES","scaffold":"The League organised a vote in Upper Silesia and then divided the territory between Germany and Poland based on the result.","reforge":{"stem":"How was Upper Silesia settled?","options":{"A":"Manchuria's resources and territory","B":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","C":"A previously demilitarised zone","D":"A plebiscite and partition"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-39","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did the Kellogg-Briand Pact renounce?","options":{"A":"Czechoslovakia from negotiations","B":"Germany and Italy","C":"War as an instrument of policy","D":"Alsace-Lorraine"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-WAR-CAUSES","scaffold":"The 1928 Kellogg-Briand Pact saw signatory states agree to renounce war as a means of settling disputes, though it had no way of enforcing this.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the 1928 pact promise?","options":{"A":"War as an instrument of policy","B":"A report condemning Japan's action","C":"The annexation of Austria","D":"Hitler was central but other failures contributed"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-40","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Why did the Depression weaken the League?","options":{"A":"They were limited","B":"Communism","C":"Invade Poland without Soviet opposition","D":"States prioritised national interests"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-WAR-CAUSES","scaffold":"As the Depression hit, countries focused on protecting their own economies and were less willing to support League actions that might cost them trade or security.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did economic crisis reduce cooperation?","options":{"A":"Peace through concessions","B":"States prioritised national interests","C":"Hitler was central but other failures contributed","D":"National self-determination"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-41","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did Japan seize in Manchuria?","options":{"A":"Manchuria's resources and territory","B":"The Treaty of Versailles","C":"Invade Poland without Soviet opposition","D":"They created financial and political resentment"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-WAR-CAUSES","scaffold":"Japan invaded Manchuria in 1931 to gain its raw materials and land, using a staged incident on the South Manchurian Railway as a pretext.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was Manchuria attractive to Japan?","options":{"A":"Alsace-Lorraine","B":"Germany and Italy","C":"Manchuria's resources and territory","D":"States prioritised national interests"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-42","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did the Lytton Commission produce?","options":{"A":"A previously demilitarised zone","B":"A report condemning Japan's action","C":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","D":"Its founding agreement"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-WAR-CAUSES","scaffold":"The Lytton Commission investigated the invasion and concluded Japan was the aggressor, though the League took no effective action against it.","reforge":{"stem":"What was the League's response to Manchuria?","options":{"A":"Communism","B":"They were limited","C":"Manchuria's resources and territory","D":"A report condemning Japan's action"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-43","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did Italy use in the Abyssinian invasion?","options":{"A":"Hitler was central but other failures contributed","B":"The annexation of Austria","C":"Modern weapons and air power","D":"The United States"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-WAR-CAUSES","scaffold":"Italy used tanks, aircraft and even poison gas against poorly equipped Abyssinian forces, giving it a huge military advantage.","reforge":{"stem":"Which advantage helped Italy conquer Abyssinia?","options":{"A":"Modern weapons and air power","B":"The Treaty of Versailles","C":"Invade Poland without Soviet opposition","D":"They created financial and political resentment"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-44","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What was the Axis formed between?","options":{"A":"Sanctions","B":"Peace through concessions","C":"National self-determination","D":"Germany and Italy"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"The 1936 Rome-Berlin Axis was an alliance between Hitler's Germany and Mussolini's Italy, formalising their cooperation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which two dictatorships formed the Axis?","options":{"A":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","B":"Germany and Italy","C":"A previously demilitarised zone","D":"Its founding agreement"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-45","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did the Anti-Comintern Pact oppose?","options":{"A":"Communism","B":"Czechoslovakia from negotiations","C":"Alsace-Lorraine","D":"A plebiscite and partition"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"The Anti-Comintern Pact, signed by Germany and Japan (later joined by Italy), was aimed at opposing the spread of Soviet-backed communism.","reforge":{"stem":"What ideology did the pact target?","options":{"A":"Hitler was central but other failures contributed","B":"The annexation of Austria","C":"Communism","D":"The United States"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-46","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did German conscription in 1935 breach?","options":{"A":"The rest of Czechoslovakia","B":"The Treaty of Versailles","C":"They were limited","D":"War as an instrument of policy"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Reintroducing conscription and building an army above the Treaty's 100,000 limit directly breached the disarmament terms Germany had agreed to in 1919.","reforge":{"stem":"Which treaty term did conscription violate?","options":{"A":"Sanctions","B":"Peace through concessions","C":"National self-determination","D":"The Treaty of Versailles"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-47","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What was the remilitarised Rhineland?","options":{"A":"They created financial and political resentment","B":"Invade Poland without Soviet opposition","C":"A previously demilitarised zone","D":"States prioritised national interests"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"The Rhineland had been declared a demilitarised zone under the Treaty of Versailles; German troops marching in was a direct treaty violation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which area did Hitler reoccupy in 1936?","options":{"A":"A previously demilitarised zone","B":"Czechoslovakia from negotiations","C":"Alsace-Lorraine","D":"A plebiscite and partition"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-48","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What was the Anschluss?","options":{"A":"Manchuria's resources and territory","B":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","C":"Its founding agreement","D":"The annexation of Austria"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"The Anschluss in March 1938 saw Germany annex Austria, uniting German-speaking peoples in defiance of the Treaty of Versailles, which had explicitly banned this union.","reforge":{"stem":"Which event united Austria with Nazi Germany?","options":{"A":"The rest of Czechoslovakia","B":"The annexation of Austria","C":"They were limited","D":"War as an instrument of policy"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-49","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did appeasement aim to preserve?","options":{"A":"Peace through concessions","B":"Hitler was central but other failures contributed","C":"The United States","D":"A report condemning Japan's action"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Appeasement was based on the idea that granting Hitler's demands, such as the Sudetenland, would satisfy him and avoid another devastating war.","reforge":{"stem":"What was the intended purpose of appeasement?","options":{"A":"They created financial and political resentment","B":"Invade Poland without Soviet opposition","C":"Peace through concessions","D":"States prioritised national interests"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-50","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did the Munich Agreement exclude?","options":{"A":"National self-determination","B":"Czechoslovakia from negotiations","C":"Sanctions","D":"A report condemning Japan's action"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-CONSEQUENCES","scaffold":"Czechoslovakia was not invited to the Munich Conference, where Britain, France, Germany and Italy decided to hand the Sudetenland to Germany without its consent.","reforge":{"stem":"Which country was not represented at Munich?","options":{"A":"Its founding agreement","B":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","C":"Manchuria's resources and territory","D":"Czechoslovakia from negotiations"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-51","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did Germany occupy after Munich?","options":{"A":"A plebiscite and partition","B":"Alsace-Lorraine","C":"The rest of Czechoslovakia","D":"Germany and Italy"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-CONSEQUENCES","scaffold":"In March 1939, Hitler broke the Munich Agreement by occupying the remaining Czech lands, proving his ambitions went beyond uniting German speakers.","reforge":{"stem":"What showed Hitler's wider ambitions in 1939?","options":{"A":"The rest of Czechoslovakia","B":"Hitler was central but other failures contributed","C":"The United States","D":"A report condemning Japan's action"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-52","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What did the Nazi-Soviet Pact allow Hitler to do?","options":{"A":"Communism","B":"They were limited","C":"They created financial and political resentment","D":"Invade Poland without Soviet opposition"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-CONSEQUENCES","scaffold":"The August 1939 Nazi-Soviet Pact included a secret agreement to divide Poland between Germany and the USSR, removing the risk of Soviet resistance to a German invasion.","reforge":{"stem":"How did the pact assist Hitler?","options":{"A":"National self-determination","B":"Invade Poland without Soviet opposition","C":"Sanctions","D":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-53","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"What was the Phoney War?","options":{"A":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","B":"They created financial and political resentment that made a wider war inevitable from the outset.","C":"States prioritised national interests","D":"The Treaty of Versailles"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-CONSEQUENCES","scaffold":"Despite Britain and France declaring war in September 1939, there was little large-scale fighting in Western Europe until Germany's invasions of Scandinavia and France in 1940 — a period nicknamed the 'Phoney War'.","reforge":{"stem":"What describes the months after September 1939?","options":{"A":"A plebiscite and partition of the disputed territory between rival national claimants.","B":"Alsace-Lorraine","C":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","D":"Germany and Italy"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-IW-54","spec":"GCSE-HIST-INTERWAR","stem":"Which judgement best explains responsibility for war?","options":{"A":"Its founding agreement","B":"Hitler was central but other failures contributed","C":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","D":"A previously demilitarised zone"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-IW-CONSEQUENCES","scaffold":"Most historians see Hitler's aggressive expansion as the main cause of war, but also point to the League's weaknesses, appeasement and the Depression as contributing factors that allowed him to act.","reforge":{"stem":"How should responsibility for war be assessed?","options":{"A":"Britain and France planned to defend Abyssinia fully","B":"They were limited","C":"Communism","D":"Hitler was central but other failures contributed"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-interwar"}]},"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH":{"label":"Paper 2 — Health and the People","color":"#a16207","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-01","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Which idea most shaped medieval explanations of disease?","options":{"A":"The Four Humours","B":"Germ theory","C":"Surgeons had no tools","D":"Vaccination"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-BELIEFS","scaffold":"Medieval physicians believed illness resulted from an imbalance of the four humours: blood, phlegm, black bile and yellow bile.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did bloodletting fit humoral theory?","options":{"A":"Doctors never treated wounds","B":"It killed all bacteria","C":"It removed excess blood","D":"It prevented plague"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-02","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What was a major limitation of medieval surgery?","options":{"A":"Surgeons had no tools","B":"There was little understanding of infection","C":"Hospitals were illegal","D":"It stopped contaminated water being consumed"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-BELIEFS","scaffold":"Surgeons could set bones and amputate, but pain, blood loss and infection made operations extremely dangerous.","reforge":{"stem":"Which development most reduced surgical pain in the nineteenth century?","options":{"A":"The NHS","B":"The Black Death","C":"Humoural theory","D":"Anaesthetics"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-03","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why did the Black Death lead some people to blame God?","options":{"A":"Observation and questioning of authority","B":"It spread only in monasteries","C":"Religion offered a familiar explanation","D":"The king ordered prayer banned"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-BELIEFS","scaffold":"Many interpreted plague as divine punishment, although other explanations included miasma and astrology.","reforge":{"stem":"What was a common medieval response to plague?","options":{"A":"Prayer and penitence","B":"Public health insurance","C":"Germ testing","D":"Mass vaccination"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-04","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did Vesalius contribute to medical knowledge?","options":{"A":"They require long-term prevention and care","B":"He invented X-rays","C":"He founded the NHS","D":"He challenged Galen through dissections"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-BELIEFS","scaffold":"Vesalius used human dissection to identify inaccuracies in Galen's anatomical ideas, though change was gradual.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Vesalius's work not transform treatment immediately?","options":{"A":"No one could read books","B":"Knowledge did not yet provide cures","C":"He opposed all observation","D":"The Church banned anatomy everywhere"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-05","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What was William Harvey's key discovery?","options":{"A":"Blood circulated around the body","B":"Cholera came from water","C":"Bacteria caused infection","D":"Church leaders had identified bacteria"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-BELIEFS","scaffold":"Harvey showed that the heart pumped blood in a continuous circulation, challenging Galen's account.","reforge":{"stem":"What helped Harvey reach his conclusions?","options":{"A":"Government welfare","B":"The Church banned anatomy everywhere","C":"Careful observation and experiment","D":"Antibiotic trials"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-06","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why was Jenner's smallpox vaccination important?","options":{"A":"He was a successful court favourite and soldier","B":"It offered protection before disease developed","C":"It created the first hospital","D":"It removed all epidemics immediately"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-BELIEFS","scaffold":"Jenner used cowpox to develop immunity to smallpox, although acceptance took time and mechanisms were not understood.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did some people resist Jenner's vaccination?","options":{"A":"It required no evidence","B":"It was free everywhere","C":"It spread only in monasteries","D":"They feared or distrusted it"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-07","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did Pasteur's germ theory propose?","options":{"A":"The Four Humours were correct","B":"It proved all disease came from miasma","C":"Disease was caused by microorganisms","D":"Only poor people caught disease"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-BELIEFS","scaffold":"Pasteur showed that microorganisms caused decay and disease, providing a new scientific explanation.","reforge":{"stem":"How did Koch strengthen germ theory?","options":{"A":"He linked specific microbes to diseases","B":"He opposed microscopes","C":"He revived bloodletting","D":"Equipment and specialist care became expensive"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-08","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why was Lister's antiseptic surgery significant?","options":{"A":"Create a fully national health service","B":"It ended all surgery","C":"It replaced anaesthetics","D":"It reduced infection in wounds"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-PREVENTION","scaffold":"Lister used carbolic acid to kill germs in operating theatres, lowering post-operative infection rates.","reforge":{"stem":"What problem did aseptic surgery later address?","options":{"A":"Poor hospital finance","B":"Keeping germs out entirely","C":"Blood-group matching","D":"It made operations painless"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-09","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why was Snow's cholera investigation important?","options":{"A":"It linked cholera to contaminated water","B":"It proved miasma theory","C":"It founded the NHS","D":"Observation and questioning of authority"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-PREVENTION","scaffold":"Snow mapped deaths around the Broad Street pump, showing the value of evidence even before germ theory was widely accepted.","reforge":{"stem":"Which action followed Snow's investigation?","options":{"A":"The Poor Law ended","B":"London banned water","C":"The pump handle was removed","D":"All homes gained antibiotics"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-10","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did the 1848 Public Health Act mainly do?","options":{"A":"They require long-term prevention and care","B":"Allow local boards to improve sanitation","C":"Make vaccination illegal","D":"Close urban housing"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-PREVENTION","scaffold":"The Act established a central board and permitted local action, but it was limited because adoption was not compulsory.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was the 1875 Public Health Act stronger?","options":{"A":"Bacteria surviving antibiotic treatment","B":"It banned sewers","C":"It ended urban growth","D":"It made sanitation measures compulsory"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-11","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why was penicillin a major medical breakthrough?","options":{"A":"It replaced all surgery","B":"Create a fully national health service","C":"It killed bacteria causing infections","D":"It prevented every cancer"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-PREVENTION","scaffold":"Penicillin became widely available after mass production during the Second World War and transformed treatment of bacterial infection.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was Fleming alone unable to transform treatment in 1928?","options":{"A":"He could not mass-produce it","B":"It removed local responsibilities","C":"He lacked a microscope","D":"He had no laboratory"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-12","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What was the main purpose of the NHS when founded in 1948?","options":{"A":"Voluntary charity hospitals only","B":"Private insurance for the wealthy","C":"Military medical training","D":"Free healthcare at point of use"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-PREVENTION","scaffold":"The NHS aimed to provide healthcare funded through taxation and available regardless of wealth.","reforge":{"stem":"Which wartime experience influenced the creation of the NHS?","options":{"A":"The League of Nations","B":"Emergency Medical Service coordination","C":"The Gunpowder Plot","D":"Bacteria surviving antibiotic treatment"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-31","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did medieval physicians use astrology to decide?","options":{"A":"Treatment timing","B":"Scientific observation and experiment","C":"Radiation to damage cancer cells","D":"Bacteria surviving antibiotic treatment"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-INDUSTRIAL","scaffold":"Medieval physicians used astrology and 'zodiac charts' to identify the most favourable time to bleed or treat a patient, based on the theory that the stars influenced the body's humours.","reforge":{"stem":"How could astrology influence medieval medicine?","options":{"A":"Bacterial infections","B":"Observation and questioning of authority","C":"Treatment timing","D":"Action against the five giants"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-32","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What was a medieval apothecary?","options":{"A":"Legal bodies for dissection","B":"A seller of medicines","C":"Reducing disease before it develops","D":"They require long-term prevention and care"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-INDUSTRIAL","scaffold":"An apothecary prepared and sold herbal remedies and medicines, acting as an early form of pharmacist that many people relied on instead of an expensive physician.","reforge":{"stem":"Who prepared and sold remedies?","options":{"A":"Universal access to treatment","B":"Medical ideas and diagrams","C":"Possibilities of modern surgery","D":"A seller of medicines"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-33","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did hospitals often provide in the Middle Ages?","options":{"A":"Infectious disease spread","B":"Nursing standards and hospital hygiene","C":"Care and religious charity","D":"Smoke pollution"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-INDUSTRIAL","scaffold":"Medieval hospitals were mostly run by the Church and focused on care, comfort and prayer for the sick and dying rather than medical treatment or cure.","reforge":{"stem":"What was a major role of medieval hospitals?","options":{"A":"Care and religious charity","B":"Scientific observation and experiment","C":"Radiation to damage cancer cells","D":"Bacteria surviving antibiotic treatment"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-34","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did the Renaissance encourage in medicine?","options":{"A":"Cheaper medicines at scale","B":"The explanation of infection","C":"It stopped contaminated water being consumed","D":"Observation and questioning of authority"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-INDUSTRIAL","scaffold":"The Renaissance encouraged direct observation and dissection rather than blind acceptance of ancient authorities like Galen, laying the groundwork for figures such as Vesalius to correct centuries-old errors.","reforge":{"stem":"How did Renaissance thinking affect medical knowledge?","options":{"A":"Legal bodies for dissection","B":"Observation and questioning of authority","C":"Reducing disease before it develops","D":"They require long-term prevention and care"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-35","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did the printing press help spread?","options":{"A":"Medical ideas and diagrams","B":"Bacterial infections","C":"Action against the five giants","D":"Equipment and specialist care became expensive"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-INDUSTRIAL","scaffold":"The printing press allowed anatomical diagrams and medical texts, such as Vesalius's work, to be copied accurately and distributed quickly across Europe.","reforge":{"stem":"How did printing support medical change?","options":{"A":"Nursing standards and hospital hygiene","B":"Infectious disease spread","C":"Medical ideas and diagrams","D":"Smoke pollution"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-36","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did the Royal Society encourage?","options":{"A":"Equipment and specialist care became expensive","B":"Scientific observation and experiment","C":"Universal access to treatment","D":"Understanding inherited disease"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-GOVERNMENT","scaffold":"Founded in 1660, the Royal Society promoted experimentation and the sharing of scientific findings, encouraging a more evidence-based approach to medicine.","reforge":{"stem":"Which institution promoted experimental science?","options":{"A":"It stopped contaminated water being consumed","B":"The explanation of infection","C":"Cheaper medicines at scale","D":"Scientific observation and experiment"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-37","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did the 1832 Anatomy Act provide?","options":{"A":"Bacteria surviving antibiotic treatment","B":"Radiation to damage cancer cells","C":"Legal bodies for dissection","D":"Benefits are not distributed equally"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-GOVERNMENT","scaffold":"The 1832 Anatomy Act gave surgeons legal access to unclaimed bodies and donated corpses for dissection, ending the illegal trade in bodies by grave-robbers like Burke and Hare.","reforge":{"stem":"How did the Anatomy Act support medical training?","options":{"A":"Legal bodies for dissection","B":"Bacterial infections","C":"Action against the five giants","D":"Equipment and specialist care became expensive"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-38","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did Florence Nightingale improve?","options":{"A":"Treatment timing","B":"Reducing disease before it develops","C":"They require long-term prevention and care","D":"Nursing standards and hospital hygiene"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-GOVERNMENT","scaffold":"Florence Nightingale's work during the Crimean War and after established better sanitation, ventilation and professional training, transforming nursing into a respected profession.","reforge":{"stem":"Which area did Nightingale reform?","options":{"A":"It stopped contaminated water being consumed","B":"Nursing standards and hospital hygiene","C":"Universal access to treatment","D":"Possibilities of modern surgery"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-39","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did the germ theory change?","options":{"A":"The explanation of infection","B":"Possibilities of modern surgery","C":"Smoke pollution","D":"A seller of medicines"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-GOVERNMENT","scaffold":"Pasteur's germ theory showed that specific microorganisms caused specific diseases, replacing older ideas like miasma (bad air) and giving doctors a scientific basis for prevention and treatment.","reforge":{"stem":"What medical idea did germ theory replace?","options":{"A":"Benefits are not distributed equally","B":"Radiation to damage cancer cells","C":"The explanation of infection","D":"Bacteria surviving antibiotic treatment"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-40","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did antibiotics treat?","options":{"A":"It stopped contaminated water being consumed","B":"Bacterial infections","C":"Cheaper medicines at scale","D":"Care and religious charity"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-GOVERNMENT","scaffold":"Antibiotics like penicillin kill or stop the growth of bacteria, making them effective against bacterial infections but not viruses.","reforge":{"stem":"Which illnesses can antibiotics target?","options":{"A":"Reducing disease before it develops","B":"Treatment timing","C":"They require long-term prevention and care","D":"Bacterial infections"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-41","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did the first heart transplant demonstrate?","options":{"A":"Equipment and specialist care became expensive","B":"Action against the five giants","C":"Possibilities of modern surgery","D":"Observation and questioning of authority"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-GOVERNMENT","scaffold":"The first successful heart transplant in 1967 showed how far surgical technique, anaesthesia and understanding of the immune system had advanced, opening up new possibilities in transplant medicine.","reforge":{"stem":"What did transplant surgery show?","options":{"A":"Possibilities of modern surgery","B":"Radiation to damage cancer cells","C":"Smoke pollution","D":"A seller of medicines"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-42","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What does radiotherapy use?","options":{"A":"Medical ideas and diagrams","B":"Universal access to treatment","C":"Benefits are not distributed equally","D":"Radiation to damage cancer cells"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Radiotherapy directs high-energy radiation at tumours to damage and destroy cancer cells while trying to limit harm to surrounding healthy tissue.","reforge":{"stem":"How can radiotherapy treat cancer?","options":{"A":"It stopped contaminated water being consumed","B":"Radiation to damage cancer cells","C":"Cheaper medicines at scale","D":"Care and religious charity"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-43","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What is preventative medicine?","options":{"A":"Reducing disease before it develops","B":"Bacteria surviving antibiotic treatment","C":"Benefits are not distributed equally","D":"Scientific observation and experiment"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Preventative medicine focuses on stopping illness occurring in the first place, through measures like vaccination, screening and public health campaigns, rather than treating disease after it appears.","reforge":{"stem":"What does prevention aim to do?","options":{"A":"Observation and questioning of authority","B":"Action against the five giants","C":"Reducing disease before it develops","D":"Equipment and specialist care became expensive"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-44","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did mass vaccination reduce?","options":{"A":"They require long-term prevention and care","B":"Infectious disease spread","C":"Treatment timing","D":"Legal bodies for dissection"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Mass vaccination programmes, such as against smallpox and measles, built up herd immunity in the population, sharply cutting the spread of infectious diseases.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a population benefit of vaccination?","options":{"A":"Universal access to treatment","B":"Medical ideas and diagrams","C":"Understanding inherited disease","D":"Infectious disease spread"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-45","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why did clean water reduce cholera?","options":{"A":"A seller of medicines","B":"Smoke pollution","C":"It stopped contaminated water being consumed","D":"Equipment and specialist care became expensive"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"John Snow's 1854 investigation linked cholera to a contaminated water pump; later improvements to sewers and clean water supply removed the main route of infection.","reforge":{"stem":"How did sanitation prevent cholera?","options":{"A":"It stopped contaminated water being consumed","B":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain","C":"Benefits are not distributed equally","D":"Scientific observation and experiment"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-46","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did the Beveridge Report recommend?","options":{"A":"Understanding inherited disease","B":"Cheaper medicines at scale","C":"Care and religious charity","D":"Action against the five giants"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"The 1942 Beveridge Report identified five 'giant evils' — Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness — and recommended a welfare state to tackle them, including a National Health Service.","reforge":{"stem":"Which report influenced post-war welfare?","options":{"A":"Treatment timing","B":"Action against the five giants","C":"They require long-term prevention and care","D":"Legal bodies for dissection"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-47","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What was one benefit of the NHS?","options":{"A":"Universal access to treatment","B":"Equipment and specialist care became expensive","C":"Observation and questioning of authority","D":"Bacterial infections"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Launched in 1948, the NHS provided medical care free at the point of use for everyone, regardless of ability to pay, ending reliance on charity or private insurance for many.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the NHS make more widely available?","options":{"A":"A seller of medicines","B":"Smoke pollution","C":"Universal access to treatment","D":"Nursing standards and hospital hygiene"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-48","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What is antibiotic resistance?","options":{"A":"It stopped contaminated water being consumed","B":"Bacteria surviving antibiotic treatment","C":"Medical ideas and diagrams","D":"Possibilities of modern surgery"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Antibiotic resistance occurs when bacteria evolve to survive drugs that once killed them, often through overuse or incomplete courses of antibiotics, making some infections harder to treat.","reforge":{"stem":"What makes some infections harder to cure?","options":{"A":"Observation and questioning of authority","B":"Cheaper medicines at scale","C":"Care and religious charity","D":"Bacteria surviving antibiotic treatment"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-49","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why do lifestyle diseases challenge modern health systems?","options":{"A":"It stopped contaminated water being consumed","B":"Benefits are not distributed equally","C":"They require long-term prevention and care","D":"Radiation to damage cancer cells"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Conditions like heart disease, diabetes and obesity develop over years and need ongoing lifestyle change and management rather than a single cure, straining health systems built around treating acute illness.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are lifestyle diseases difficult to manage?","options":{"A":"They require long-term prevention and care","B":"Equipment and specialist care became expensive","C":"Observation and questioning of authority","D":"Bacterial infections"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-50","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did the Clean Air Act of 1956 target?","options":{"A":"Reducing disease before it develops","B":"Treatment timing","C":"Legal bodies for dissection","D":"Smoke pollution"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"The Clean Air Act followed the deadly 1952 London smog and restricted the burning of coal in urban areas, reducing air pollution and related respiratory illness.","reforge":{"stem":"Which environmental health problem did it address?","options":{"A":"Understanding inherited disease","B":"Smoke pollution","C":"Medical ideas and diagrams","D":"Possibilities of modern surgery"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-51","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did mass production make possible?","options":{"A":"Cheaper medicines at scale","B":"A seller of medicines","C":"Nursing standards and hospital hygiene","D":"Infectious disease spread"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Mass production techniques allowed drugs like penicillin to be manufactured in bulk during and after the Second World War, dramatically lowering their cost and making them widely available.","reforge":{"stem":"How did industrial production affect treatment?","options":{"A":"Scientific observation and experiment","B":"Benefits are not distributed equally","C":"Cheaper medicines at scale","D":"Radiation to damage cancer cells"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-52","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why did medical technology increase costs?","options":{"A":"Care and religious charity","B":"Equipment and specialist care became expensive","C":"The explanation of infection","D":"He was a successful court favourite and soldier"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Advances such as MRI scanners, transplant surgery and specialist cancer treatment require expensive equipment and highly trained staff, driving up the overall cost of modern healthcare.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can modern treatment place pressure on budgets?","options":{"A":"Hitler was central but other failures contributed","B":"Treatment timing","C":"Legal bodies for dissection","D":"Equipment and specialist care became expensive"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-53","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"What did DNA research contribute to medicine?","options":{"A":"Bacterial infections","B":"Observation and questioning of authority","C":"Understanding inherited disease","D":"Action against the five giants"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"The discovery of DNA's structure in 1953 and later genetic research helped explain how conditions are inherited, enabling genetic testing and, eventually, targeted treatments.","reforge":{"stem":"How can genetics improve diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Understanding inherited disease","B":"A seller of medicines","C":"Nursing standards and hospital hygiene","D":"Infectious disease spread"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-54","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why does access remain important despite medical progress?","options":{"A":"Universal access to treatment","B":"Medical ideas and diagrams","C":"Scientific observation and experiment","D":"Benefits are not distributed equally"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Despite medical advances, access to the best treatment and outcomes still varies by income, location and other social factors, meaning progress has not benefited everyone equally.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can health inequalities persist?","options":{"A":"Care and religious charity","B":"Benefits are not distributed equally","C":"The explanation of infection","D":"It stopped contaminated water being consumed"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-13","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why did public health improve in industrial Britain after 1875?","options":{"A":"Compulsory sanitation and cleaner water","B":"Less urban growth","C":"They require long-term prevention and care","D":"Humoural theory"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-PREVENTION","scaffold":"The 1875 Act required local authorities to provide clean water, sewers and rubbish collection.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the building of sewers mainly reduce?","options":{"A":"Medical training","B":"The need for hospitals","C":"Water-borne disease","D":"The use of anaesthetics"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-14","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why did the germ theory not immediately improve all public health?","options":{"A":"Britain and France planned to defend Abyssinia fully","B":"It did not automatically provide infrastructure","C":"It ended sanitation","D":"It was based on astrology"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-PREVENTION","scaffold":"Scientific explanations had to be converted into practical measures such as clean water, sewage systems and hygiene.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change applied germ theory in hospitals?","options":{"A":"Humour balancing","B":"It ended all surgery","C":"Less handwashing","D":"Antiseptic cleaning"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-15","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why did blood transfusion become safer in the twentieth century?","options":{"A":"Hospitals closed","B":"Operations became unnecessary","C":"Blood groups were identified","D":"Germs disappeared"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-MEDICINE","scaffold":"Landsteiner's identification of blood groups helped doctors match donors and patients more safely.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the discovery of blood groups reduce?","options":{"A":"Dangerous transfusion reactions","B":"The need for nurses","C":"The use of surgery","D":"To make private insurance illegal"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-16","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why did surgery advance rapidly during the twentieth century?","options":{"A":"The Four Humours returned","B":"Doctors abandoned hospitals","C":"Operations became unnecessary once vaccination eliminated the diseases that caused them.","D":"Anaesthetics, antiseptics and better technology combined"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-MEDICINE","scaffold":"Several developments worked together: pain control, infection prevention, blood management, imaging and improved surgical techniques.","reforge":{"stem":"What did X-rays allow doctors to do?","options":{"A":"Kill bacteria directly","B":"See internal bones and injuries","C":"Replace all operations","D":"Everyone received unlimited care"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-17","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why did vaccination programmes face opposition?","options":{"A":"Some people distrusted compulsion and medical risks","B":"Britain and France planned to defend Abyssinia fully","C":"They increased smallpox","D":"Doctors preferred plague"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-MEDICINE","scaffold":"Opposition included concerns about compulsion, safety, liberty and religious or personal beliefs, even though vaccination reduced disease.","reforge":{"stem":"What was the effect of compulsory vaccination laws?","options":{"A":"All treatments were equally available","B":"No change in vaccination","C":"Higher uptake but continued protest","D":"The abolition of public health"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-18","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why did the government introduce National Insurance in 1911?","options":{"A":"To replace all hospitals","B":"To provide limited help with sickness and unemployment","C":"To fund medieval medicine","D":"To make private insurance illegal and replace it entirely with state provision."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-MEDICINE","scaffold":"National Insurance provided contributory support, reflecting growing government responsibility for welfare and health.","reforge":{"stem":"What was a limitation of early National Insurance?","options":{"A":"It applied only to monarchs","B":"All treatments were equally available","C":"It ended unemployment","D":"Coverage and benefits were limited"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-19","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"How did the First World War influence medical treatment?","options":{"A":"It prevented prosthetics","B":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","C":"It encouraged advances in surgery and rehabilitation","D":"It restored miasma theory"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-MEDICINE","scaffold":"Large numbers of wounded soldiers encouraged developments in plastic surgery, blood transfusion and rehabilitation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did the war encourage blood transfusion?","options":{"A":"Large-scale wounds required it","B":"The end of smallpox immediately","C":"Anaesthetics were banned","D":"Doctors stopped operating"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-20","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why did health improve unevenly during the twentieth century?","options":{"A":"Disease affected only cities","B":"They require long-term prevention and care","C":"Public health stopped changing","D":"Income, housing and access still differed"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-MEDICINE","scaffold":"Medical advances did not remove inequalities caused by poverty, housing, diet, work and access to services.","reforge":{"stem":"Which factor can still affect health today?","options":{"A":"No access to information","B":"Living conditions and income","C":"The abolition of public health","D":"The medieval humours alone"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-21","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Why did the NHS face pressure after 1948?","options":{"A":"Demand rose as treatments and expectations expanded","B":"Nobody used hospitals","C":"Britain and France planned to defend Abyssinia fully","D":"It abolished doctors"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-MEDICINE","scaffold":"An ageing population, expensive technology, wider expectations and chronic disease increased demand on a tax-funded service.","reforge":{"stem":"What principle distinguishes the NHS from private insurance?","options":{"A":"Doctors work without training","B":"Only wealthy patients qualify","C":"Care is primarily based on need","D":"Patients pay every hospital bill"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-HP-22","spec":"GCSE-HIST-HEALTH","stem":"Which factor most explains the continuing importance of public health?","options":{"A":"Hospitals cannot treat illness effectively without prevention programmes running alongside them.","B":"Prevention can reduce disease before treatment is needed","C":"Doctors no longer use science","D":"All diseases are inherited"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-HEALTH-PREVENTION","scaffold":"Vaccination, clean environments, health education and screening can prevent illness or identify it earlier.","reforge":{"stem":"What is an example of preventative medicine?","options":{"A":"Amputation after injury","B":"Pain relief during an operation","C":"Emergency surgery","D":"Immunisation"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-health"}]},"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH":{"label":"Paper 2 — Elizabethan England","color":"#92400e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-01","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why was Elizabeth's marriage a political issue?","options":{"A":"It affected succession and foreign alliances","B":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain","C":"She had no relatives","D":"England had no rivals"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-RELIGION","scaffold":"Marriage could secure an heir and alliance, but also risked foreign influence and loss of Elizabeth's authority.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Elizabeth avoid naming a successor?","options":{"A":"She had already chosen Mary Queen of Scots","B":"There were no possible heirs","C":"It could encourage plots and rivalry","D":"Parliament forbade succession"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-02","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What was the aim of the Religious Settlement of 1559?","options":{"A":"A Catholic-backed attempt against Elizabeth","B":"Create a Protestant church with compromise","C":"Abolish all church services","D":"Give the Pope control of England"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-RELIGION","scaffold":"The Settlement made Elizabeth Supreme Governor and established Protestant worship, while retaining some traditional features.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Puritans criticise the Settlement?","options":{"A":"It was too Protestant","B":"She had already chosen Mary Queen of Scots","C":"It banned the Bible","D":"It kept too much Catholic ceremony"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-03","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did the Northern Rebellion of 1569 threaten Elizabeth?","options":{"A":"She had already chosen Mary Queen of Scots","B":"Spain had invaded London","C":"Catholic nobles sought to replace her","D":"Parliament ended taxation"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-RELIGION","scaffold":"Catholic northern nobles aimed to free Mary, Queen of Scots and restore Catholic influence.","reforge":{"stem":"What did Elizabeth's response to the rebellion show?","options":{"A":"She acted firmly against threats","B":"She would tolerate armed rebellion","C":"She supported Mary Queen of Scots","D":"She abolished noble power"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-04","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why was Mary, Queen of Scots, a danger to Elizabeth?","options":{"A":"She led the Spanish Armada","B":"She had already chosen Mary Queen of Scots","C":"She ruled England as regent","D":"She was a Catholic claimant to the throne"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-RELIGION","scaffold":"Mary's Catholic faith and claim to the English throne made her a focus for plots against Elizabeth.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was Mary executed in 1587?","options":{"A":"Elizabeth refused to name an heir publicly","B":"Evidence linked her to the Babington Plot","C":"She refused a royal marriage","D":"She supported the Puritans"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-05","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What was a main cause of increased poverty in Elizabethan England?","options":{"A":"Population growth and rising prices","B":"Universal free education","C":"The end of enclosure","D":"She had already chosen Mary Queen of Scots"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-RELIGION","scaffold":"Population growth, inflation, enclosure and poor harvests increased pressure on jobs, food and housing.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the 1601 Poor Law require parishes to do?","options":{"A":"Fund overseas exploration","B":"Abolish all taxation","C":"Provide local poor relief","D":"Close theatres"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-06","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did government regulate Elizabethan theatres?","options":{"A":"It wanted to ban all drama","B":"It feared disorder and disease","C":"It opposed Shakespeare's writing","D":"It needed actors for the navy"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-RELIGION","scaffold":"Authorities worried that theatres attracted crowds, crime and plague, although theatre remained popular and commercially important.","reforge":{"stem":"Who protected many acting companies?","options":{"A":"The Pope","B":"Local parish priests","C":"Foreign ambassadors","D":"Noble patrons"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-07","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did Elizabeth support voyages of exploration?","options":{"A":"She had already chosen Mary Queen of Scots","B":"To close English ports","C":"To seek trade, wealth and influence","D":"To end naval development"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-RELIGION","scaffold":"Exploration promised new trade routes, resources and prestige, while challenging Spanish power.","reforge":{"stem":"What was a major difficulty faced by the Roanoke colony?","options":{"A":"Conflict and lack of supplies","B":"Support from all local peoples","C":"No Atlantic crossing","D":"Too much royal funding"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-08","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did relations with Spain worsen during Elizabeth's reign?","options":{"A":"It showed Elizabeth faced no opposition","B":"Spain accepted English piracy","C":"Elizabeth married Philip II","D":"Religious and commercial rivalry grew"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-QUEEN","scaffold":"Protestant England, attacks on Spanish shipping and support for Dutch rebels increased tension with Catholic Spain.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Elizabeth aid the Dutch rebels in 1585?","options":{"A":"A Catholic-backed attempt against Elizabeth","B":"To protect trade and oppose Spanish power","C":"To restore Catholicism","D":"To invade Scotland"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-09","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What was the Spanish Armada's main objective in 1588?","options":{"A":"Transport an invasion force to England","B":"Colonise North America","C":"Capture France for Elizabeth","D":"It showed Elizabeth faced no opposition"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-QUEEN","scaffold":"The Armada was intended to escort the Duke of Parma's army across the Channel to invade England.","reforge":{"stem":"Which factor helped defeat the Armada?","options":{"A":"The Long Parliament","B":"England supported Catholic missions","C":"English fire ships and weather","D":"The Battle of Waterloo"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-10","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why was the Essex Rebellion of 1601 significant?","options":{"A":"It demonstrated continuing naval strength","B":"It exposed weakness among some courtiers","C":"It restored Catholicism","D":"It made Essex king"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-QUEEN","scaffold":"Essex's failed rising showed the dangers of faction at court, but its quick collapse also showed Elizabeth's authority remained strong.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did the Essex Rebellion fail?","options":{"A":"Essex gained wide public support","B":"Elizabeth fled England","C":"Spain supplied his army","D":"Londoners did not join him"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-11","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What was patronage at Elizabeth's court?","options":{"A":"It gave Mary Queen of Scots the crown","B":"A tax on poor people","C":"The granting of favour and offices","D":"A naval battle tactic"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-QUEEN","scaffold":"Elizabeth used patronage to reward service and manage ambitious courtiers, strengthening her control at court.","reforge":{"stem":"Why could patronage strengthen a monarch?","options":{"A":"It rewarded loyalty","B":"It ended Parliament","C":"It removed all rivalries","D":"It abolished local government"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-12","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why was the 1559 Act of Supremacy important?","options":{"A":"It made England Catholic","B":"It gave Mary Queen of Scots the crown","C":"It ended Parliament","D":"It made Elizabeth Supreme Governor"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-QUEEN","scaffold":"The Act established Elizabeth as Supreme Governor of the Church of England, rejecting papal authority.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was the title 'Supreme Governor' useful?","options":{"A":"It pleased every Catholic","B":"It avoided calling a woman 'Supreme Head'","C":"It gave power to the Pope","D":"Elizabeth refused to name an heir publicly"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-31","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What was the role of the Privy Council?","options":{"A":"Advising and administering government","B":"A Catholic plot involving Mary and Spain","C":"Punishing and controlling vagrancy","D":"Military and naval campaigns were costly"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-ARMADA","scaffold":"The Privy Council was a group of Elizabeth's closest advisers who helped administer government business, oversee law and order, and advise the queen on policy.","reforge":{"stem":"What body helped Elizabeth govern?","options":{"A":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain","B":"A law regulating church worship","C":"Advising and administering government","D":"Some rural people lost common rights"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-32","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why was William Cecil important?","options":{"A":"The peace ending war with Spain","B":"He was Elizabeth's leading minister","C":"A purpose-built theatre","D":"A Catholic-backed attempt against Elizabeth"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-ARMADA","scaffold":"William Cecil (Lord Burghley) served as Elizabeth's principal secretary and most trusted adviser for most of her reign, shaping government policy on finance, religion and foreign affairs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which minister helped manage the realm?","options":{"A":"Attendance at Anglican services","B":"It demonstrated continuing naval strength","C":"Food shortages and rising prices","D":"He was Elizabeth's leading minister"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-33","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What did Elizabeth's coronation settlement establish?","options":{"A":"They associated them with immorality","B":"A plan to assassinate Elizabeth","C":"Her authority as Protestant monarch","D":"To strengthen loyalty and royal image"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-ARMADA","scaffold":"The 1559 Religious Settlement established Elizabeth as Supreme Governor of the Church of England, confirming a moderate Protestant church and her authority over it.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the early settlement reinforce?","options":{"A":"Her authority as Protestant monarch","B":"A Catholic plot involving Mary and Spain","C":"Punishing and controlling vagrancy","D":"Military and naval campaigns were costly"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-34","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What was the Act of Uniformity?","options":{"A":"Treated missionary priests as traitors","B":"Victory over Spain","C":"Spanish shipping","D":"A law regulating church worship"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-ARMADA","scaffold":"The Act of Uniformity required everyone to attend Church of England services and use the same prayer book, enforcing conformity to the new religious settlement.","reforge":{"stem":"Which Act standardised Anglican services?","options":{"A":"A purpose-built theatre","B":"A law regulating church worship","C":"A Catholic-backed attempt against Elizabeth","D":"The peace ending war with Spain"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-35","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What did recusancy fines encourage?","options":{"A":"Attendance at Anglican services","B":"Some rural people lost common rights","C":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain","D":"He was a successful court favourite and soldier"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-SOCIETY","scaffold":"Fines for recusancy (refusing to attend Anglican services) were designed to pressure Catholics into at least outwardly conforming to the state church.","reforge":{"stem":"What was the purpose of recusancy fines?","options":{"A":"They associated them with immorality","B":"A plan to assassinate Elizabeth","C":"Attendance at Anglican services","D":"To strengthen loyalty and royal image"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-36","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What was the Ridolfi Plot?","options":{"A":"He was executed","B":"A Catholic plot involving Mary and Spain","C":"It demonstrated continuing naval strength","D":"Food shortages and rising prices"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-SOCIETY","scaffold":"The 1571 Ridolfi Plot aimed to assassinate Elizabeth and place Mary, Queen of Scots on the throne with Spanish military support.","reforge":{"stem":"Which plot aimed to replace Elizabeth?","options":{"A":"Victory over Spain","B":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain","C":"Spanish shipping","D":"A Catholic plot involving Mary and Spain"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-37","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What was the Throckmorton Plot?","options":{"A":"Military and naval campaigns were costly","B":"Punishing and controlling vagrancy","C":"A Catholic-backed attempt against Elizabeth","D":"Foreign goods became easier to sell in America"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-SOCIETY","scaffold":"The 1583 Throckmorton Plot involved a planned French Catholic invasion to overthrow Elizabeth and restore Catholicism, again centred on Mary, Queen of Scots.","reforge":{"stem":"Which plot led to harsher anti-Catholic laws?","options":{"A":"A Catholic-backed attempt against Elizabeth","B":"Some rural people lost common rights","C":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain","D":"He was a successful court favourite and soldier"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-38","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What was the Babington Plot?","options":{"A":"A purpose-built theatre","B":"Advising and administering government","C":"The peace ending war with Spain","D":"A plan to assassinate Elizabeth"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-SOCIETY","scaffold":"The 1586 Babington Plot was uncovered by Walsingham's spy network and directly implicated Mary, Queen of Scots in a plan to kill Elizabeth, leading to Mary's execution.","reforge":{"stem":"Which plot provided evidence against Mary?","options":{"A":"Food shortages and rising prices","B":"A plan to assassinate Elizabeth","C":"It demonstrated continuing naval strength","D":"He was executed"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-39","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What did the Act against Jesuits do?","options":{"A":"Treated missionary priests as traitors","B":"They associated them with immorality","C":"It demonstrated continuing naval strength","D":"He was Elizabeth's leading minister"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-SOCIETY","scaffold":"The 1585 Act against Jesuits made it treason for Catholic priests trained abroad to enter England, reflecting fears that missionaries were part of a wider plot against the queen.","reforge":{"stem":"How did Parliament respond to missionary activity?","options":{"A":"Military and naval campaigns were costly","B":"Punishing and controlling vagrancy","C":"Treated missionary priests as traitors","D":"Elizabeth refused to name an heir publicly"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-40","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did enclosure cause hardship?","options":{"A":"To strengthen loyalty and royal image","B":"Some rural people lost common rights","C":"Victory over Spain","D":"Spanish shipping"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-SOCIETY","scaffold":"Enclosure fenced off land previously used communally for grazing and gathering, forcing many poorer villagers off land they depended on for their livelihood.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did enclosure increase poverty for some?","options":{"A":"The peace ending war with Spain","B":"A purpose-built theatre","C":"Advising and administering government","D":"Some rural people lost common rights"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-41","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What did the 1590s harvest failures cause?","options":{"A":"He was a successful court favourite and soldier","B":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain","C":"Food shortages and rising prices","D":"A law regulating church worship"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"A series of poor harvests in the 1590s reduced grain supplies, driving up food prices and causing real hardship, especially for the poor.","reforge":{"stem":"What followed poor harvests?","options":{"A":"Food shortages and rising prices","B":"They associated them with immorality","C":"To strengthen loyalty and royal image","D":"He was Elizabeth's leading minister"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-42","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What was the purpose of the 1572 Vagabonds Act?","options":{"A":"It demonstrated continuing naval strength","B":"Attendance at Anglican services","C":"He was executed","D":"Punishing and controlling vagrancy"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"The 1572 Vagabonds Act allowed harsh punishment of unemployed wanderers, reflecting fears that a rising population of poor, mobile people threatened social order.","reforge":{"stem":"Which problem did the Act address?","options":{"A":"Spanish shipping","B":"Punishing and controlling vagrancy","C":"Victory over Spain","D":"Her authority as Protestant monarch"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-43","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What did Shakespeare's Globe provide?","options":{"A":"A purpose-built theatre","B":"Military and naval campaigns were costly","C":"Elizabeth refused to name an heir publicly","D":"A Catholic plot involving Mary and Spain"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"The Globe, built in 1599, was a dedicated venue for staging plays including Shakespeare's, reflecting the growing popularity and professionalism of Elizabethan theatre.","reforge":{"stem":"Which venue became associated with Shakespeare?","options":{"A":"He was a successful court favourite and soldier","B":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain","C":"A purpose-built theatre","D":"A law regulating church worship"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-44","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did Puritans dislike theatres?","options":{"A":"A Catholic-backed attempt against Elizabeth","B":"They associated them with immorality","C":"Advising and administering government","D":"The peace ending war with Spain"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Puritans saw theatres as encouraging idleness, immoral behaviour and disorder, and campaigned to have them closed or restricted, especially within the City of London.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did some Puritans oppose drama?","options":{"A":"He was executed","B":"It demonstrated continuing naval strength","C":"Attendance at Anglican services","D":"They associated them with immorality"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-45","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What did Elizabethan privateers attack?","options":{"A":"He was Elizabeth's leading minister","B":"To strengthen loyalty and royal image","C":"Spanish shipping","D":"A plan to assassinate Elizabeth"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Privateers such as Francis Drake were effectively licensed pirates who attacked Spanish treasure ships returning from the Americas, both enriching England and provoking Spain.","reforge":{"stem":"What was a target of Drake and Hawkins?","options":{"A":"Spanish shipping","B":"Military and naval campaigns were costly","C":"Elizabeth refused to name an heir publicly","D":"A Catholic plot involving Mary and Spain"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-46","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did Elizabeth knight Francis Drake?","options":{"A":"Victory over Spain","B":"He was a successful court favourite and soldier","C":"Her authority as Protestant monarch","D":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Drake became the first Englishman to sail around the world and later played a key role in the fight against the Spanish Armada, earning Elizabeth's knighthood.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was Drake rewarded?","options":{"A":"The peace ending war with Spain","B":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain","C":"Advising and administering government","D":"They created financial and political resentment"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-47","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What was the significance of the English defeat at Cadiz in 1596?","options":{"A":"It demonstrated continuing naval strength","B":"He was a successful court favourite and soldier","C":"A law regulating church worship","D":"Some rural people lost common rights"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Despite the failed raid on Cadiz in 1596, the attempt showed England could still mount ambitious naval operations against Spain even after the Armada's defeat.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the Cadiz expedition show?","options":{"A":"A plan to assassinate Elizabeth","B":"Elizabeth refused to name an heir publicly","C":"It demonstrated continuing naval strength","D":"He was Elizabeth's leading minister"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-48","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did the war with Spain damage finances?","options":{"A":"It demonstrated continuing naval strength","B":"Military and naval campaigns were costly","C":"Attendance at Anglican services","D":"He was executed"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Prolonged war with Spain, including naval expeditions and the Netherlands campaign, drained crown finances throughout the later years of Elizabeth's reign.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did the conflict strain the treasury?","options":{"A":"Victory over Spain","B":"A Catholic-backed attempt against Elizabeth","C":"Her authority as Protestant monarch","D":"Military and naval campaigns were costly"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-49","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What was the Treaty of London in 1604?","options":{"A":"A Catholic plot involving Mary and Spain","B":"Elizabeth refused to name an heir publicly","C":"The peace ending war with Spain","D":"Punishing and controlling vagrancy"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"The 1604 Treaty of London, agreed after Elizabeth's death under James I, formally ended nearly two decades of war between England and Spain.","reforge":{"stem":"Which agreement ended the conflict?","options":{"A":"The peace ending war with Spain","B":"He was a successful court favourite and soldier","C":"A law regulating church worship","D":"Some rural people lost common rights"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-50","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did Elizabeth use propaganda?","options":{"A":"Advising and administering government","B":"A purpose-built theatre","C":"A Catholic-backed attempt against Elizabeth","D":"To strengthen loyalty and royal image"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Elizabeth used portraits, progresses and public ceremony to project an image of strength and legitimacy, encouraging loyalty at a time when a female, Protestant monarch faced real threats.","reforge":{"stem":"Why were royal portraits politically useful?","options":{"A":"Attendance at Anglican services","B":"To strengthen loyalty and royal image","C":"He was executed","D":"Military and naval campaigns were costly"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-51","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What did the Armada portrait celebrate?","options":{"A":"Victory over Spain","B":"He was Elizabeth's leading minister","C":"A plan to assassinate Elizabeth","D":"They associated them with immorality"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"The Armada Portrait, showing Elizabeth with the Spanish fleet in the background, celebrated England's defeat of the Spanish Armada in 1588 as a triumph of her reign.","reforge":{"stem":"What event did the portrait commemorate?","options":{"A":"Punishing and controlling vagrancy","B":"Elizabeth refused to name an heir publicly","C":"Victory over Spain","D":"A Catholic plot involving Mary and Spain"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-52","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why was Essex popular before his rebellion?","options":{"A":"Spanish shipping","B":"He was a successful court favourite and soldier","C":"Hitler was central but other failures contributed","D":"Her authority as Protestant monarch"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"The Earl of Essex was a charismatic military commander and close favourite of the ageing queen, which made his later fall from favour and rebellion in 1601 so dramatic.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Essex have influence?","options":{"A":"Advising and administering government","B":"A purpose-built theatre","C":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war","D":"He was a successful court favourite and soldier"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-53","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What happened to Essex after his rebellion?","options":{"A":"Some rural people lost common rights","B":"A law regulating church worship","C":"He was executed","D":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Essex was tried for treason after his failed rebellion against the queen's government and executed in 1601.","reforge":{"stem":"What was the result of Essex's rising?","options":{"A":"He was executed","B":"He was Elizabeth's leading minister","C":"A plan to assassinate Elizabeth","D":"They associated them with immorality"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-54","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did succession remain unresolved at Elizabeth's death?","options":{"A":"Attendance at Anglican services","B":"He was a successful court favourite and soldier","C":"Food shortages and rising prices","D":"Elizabeth refused to name an heir publicly"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Elizabeth never married or publicly named a successor, leaving the succession uncertain and causing anxiety at court right up until her death, when James VI of Scotland succeeded her.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was the succession uncertain?","options":{"A":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain","B":"Elizabeth refused to name an heir publicly","C":"Her authority as Protestant monarch","D":"Spanish shipping"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-13","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did Elizabeth use progresses around England?","options":{"A":"To display royal authority and meet subjects","B":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain","C":"To abandon London","D":"To support Spain"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-QUEEN","scaffold":"Royal progresses allowed Elizabeth to project authority, reward communities and strengthen the personal relationship between monarch and subjects.","reforge":{"stem":"What did a royal progress often require local communities to provide?","options":{"A":"A foreign army","B":"A new navy","C":"Hospitality and entertainment","D":"End the Dutch revolt peacefully"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-14","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did Parliament matter to Elizabeth's government?","options":{"A":"He was a successful court favourite and soldier","B":"It granted taxation and discussed legislation","C":"It commanded every army","D":"It appointed the Pope"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-CHALLENGES","scaffold":"Elizabeth needed parliamentary taxation for extraordinary expenses, while MPs sometimes raised religious and succession concerns.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Elizabeth resist parliamentary debate about marriage?","options":{"A":"She had no court","B":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain","C":"She opposed all laws","D":"She considered succession a royal prerogative"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-15","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"What encouraged the growth of the Elizabethan theatre?","options":{"A":"The collapse of London","B":"A Catholic-backed attempt against Elizabeth","C":"Commercial audiences and wealthy patrons","D":"Spanish censorship"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-CHALLENGES","scaffold":"Purpose-built theatres and paying audiences made drama commercially successful, while patrons also supported companies.","reforge":{"stem":"Why were theatres built outside London's city walls?","options":{"A":"The city authorities restricted them","B":"Audiences lived abroad","C":"Actors feared the sea","D":"She wanted Parliament dissolved forever"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-16","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did poverty become a concern for Elizabethan governments?","options":{"A":"There were no harvests anywhere in England throughout the whole of Elizabeth's reign.","B":"The population declined sharply","C":"All workers became wealthy","D":"Vagrancy and poor relief placed pressure on communities"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-CHALLENGES","scaffold":"Authorities feared disorder and wanted parishes to organise relief while discouraging able-bodied vagrancy.","reforge":{"stem":"How did the 1598 and 1601 Poor Laws develop policy?","options":{"A":"They banned charity","B":"They organised parish responsibility","C":"They ended all poverty","D":"They wanted everyone to travel freely"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-17","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did Elizabethan authorities regulate vagabonds?","options":{"A":"They feared disorder and wanted able-bodied people to work","B":"They wanted everyone to travel freely between parishes without needing any permission.","C":"They opposed all farming","D":"They needed more pirates"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-CHALLENGES","scaffold":"Vagrancy was associated with crime and disorder; laws distinguished people judged able to work from those needing relief.","reforge":{"stem":"What was one risk of harsh vagrancy laws?","options":{"A":"Poverty disappeared","B":"There were no harvests anywhere","C":"Genuine need could be punished","D":"Theatres became free"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-18","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why was the defeat of the Armada important for Elizabeth?","options":{"A":"It stopped privateering","B":"It strengthened national confidence and her authority","C":"It made Spain an ally","D":"A period of limited fighting after declarations of war"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-CHALLENGES","scaffold":"The victory was celebrated as a Protestant and English triumph, strengthening Elizabeth's image despite the continuing war.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did the Armada fail to invade England?","options":{"A":"He was a successful court favourite and soldier","B":"Spain had no ships","C":"Elizabeth surrendered","D":"English attacks and bad weather disrupted it"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-19","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did England's war with Spain continue after 1588?","options":{"A":"England stopped trading","B":"Spain had been conquered","C":"Neither side achieved a decisive settlement","D":"His circumnavigation and service against Spain"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-CHALLENGES","scaffold":"Privateering, attacks on Spanish possessions and failed English expeditions prolonged conflict after the Armada.","reforge":{"stem":"What was a consequence of the war with Spain?","options":{"A":"Financial pressure on the government","B":"The end of taxation","C":"She wanted Parliament dissolved forever","D":"The abolition of the navy"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-20","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did Puritanism remain a challenge after the Religious Settlement?","options":{"A":"A Catholic-backed attempt against Elizabeth","B":"Puritans controlled Spain","C":"Puritans rejected the Bible","D":"Puritans wanted further changes to worship"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-CHALLENGES","scaffold":"Puritans accepted Protestantism but objected to bishops, vestments and ceremonies retained by the Settlement.","reforge":{"stem":"How did Elizabeth respond to Puritan pressure?","options":{"A":"She abolished the Church of England and restored papal authority across the realm.","B":"She enforced conformity while allowing limited variation","C":"She made England Catholic","D":"She gave Puritans Parliament"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-21","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did Catholic plots become more serious after the arrival of missionaries?","options":{"A":"Missionaries strengthened Catholic worship and opposition","B":"They supported the Religious Settlement and rejected all missionary activity.","C":"They ended foreign support","D":"They controlled the navy"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-FOREIGN","scaffold":"Jesuit missionaries encouraged recusant Catholics and could be viewed by the government as a link to foreign intervention.","reforge":{"stem":"What was a recusant?","options":{"A":"A theatre owner","B":"Britain and France planned to defend Abyssinia fully","C":"A Catholic who refused to attend Anglican services","D":"A Spanish sailor"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"},{"id":"GCSE-HIST-EL-22","spec":"GCSE-HIST-ELIZABETH","stem":"Why did Elizabeth's government use spies?","options":{"A":"To replace Parliament","B":"To detect plots and threats to security","C":"To collect harvests","D":"A Catholic plot involving Mary and Spain"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GHIST-ELIZ-FOREIGN","scaffold":"Walsingham's network gathered intelligence about Catholic plots, foreign powers and possible rebels.","reforge":{"stem":"What was the purpose of the Bond of Association?","options":{"A":"To support Spain","B":"Return England to Roman Catholicism","C":"To fund theatres","D":"To deter plots against Elizabeth"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-gcse-hist-elizabeth"}]}});
